 also incorrectly makes valine and alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid as stoichiometric co-products rather than alternative outcomes of reduction versus reductive amination.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Either remove rxn_009 or decompose it into explicit pyruvate dimerization, reductoisomerization, and dehydration steps with literature support\",\n        \"Replace the Biochemical environment with a geochemically defined setting and justify transfer into the hydrothermal system\",\n        \"Split rxn_010 into separate competing reactions: alpha-KIV + NH3 + H2 -> valine + H2O and alpha-KIV + H2 -> alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid, with Ni catalyst, pH 8, and H2 conditions\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.06,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.34\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.19,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.36,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.24,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.43,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.26,\n      \"feedback\": \"Using Fe-Ni meteoritic material is geochemically imaginable and literature-backed for the final reductive amination step, but the pathway still depends on the same unsupported rxn_009. As written, rxn_011 also bundles valine formation and alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid formation into one impossible stoichiometric event rather than treating them as competing product channels.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Do not treat pyruvate -> alpha-KIV as a single reaction; provide explicit nonenzymatic steps or remove the branch\",\n        \"Specify the meteoritic catalyst context and transport logic into the hydrothermal setting\",\n        \"Split rxn_011 into separate competing valine and alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid reactions with explicit Fe-Ni catalyst and H2 conditions\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"best_pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n  \"overall_confidence\": 0.43,\n  \"network_feedback\": \"The network is connected, has sufficient size, and is well aligned with the literature at the strategic level: it correctly treats valine as skeleton-limited, includes the two best-supported end-games (Strecker from isobutyraldehyde and metal-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate), and explicitly retains the pantoate and alpha-hydroxyisovalerate side sinks plus the unresolved alpha-KIV bottleneck. However, as an encoded reaction network it is not valid enough for approval. The deterministic balance check finds 11 of 12 reactions unbalanced; only rxn_004 is balanced. That alone severely lowers network validity and chemical feasibility. The main repair priorities are: rxn_001-rxn_003 should be replaced by balanced spark/plume product-generation reactions or by a separate product-pool abstraction with explicit transfer, and the checker suggests H2 is likely among the coproducts if these are retained as overall stoichiometries; rxn_003 is also labeled sulfur-rich despite having no sulfur-containing reactant. rxn_006-rxn_007 should release H2O as Strecker byproduct. rxn_008 should be written as mol_008 + 2 mol_003 -> mol_012 + mol_002 (or NH4+ equivalent). rxn_005 should not be patched with the checker's generic O2/CO2 hints; instead it needs to be decomposed into the actual Patel-Sutherland substeps with HS-/H2S, Cu photoredox, UV, and staged delivery. rxn_009 is both unbalanced and unsupported by current literature and should be decomposed into explicit referenced steps or removed. rxn_010-rxn_011 incorrectly combine valine and alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid as co-products; they should be split into separate competing reactions with catalyst, pH, and H2 conditions. rxn_012 likely needs added water and NH3 release after cyanide capture/hydrolysis. Also fix the mol_008 structure or InChI inconsistency and add reaction-level citations tying individual nodes to the cited studies. No pathway is approvable in its current formalization, even though the conceptual literature framing is good.\"\n}", "parsed_response": {"pathway_evaluations": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_001": 0.28, "rxn_006": 0.6, "rxn_008": 0.48}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.45, "pathway_coherence": 0.73, "environmental_progression": 0.63, "target_achievement": 0.62, "overall_confidence": 0.54, "feedback": "Conceptually aligned with the classic Miller-to-Strecker route and with literature reporting valine in spark-discharge products. The pathway is weakened because rxn_001 is an unbalanced product-pool shorthand rather than a chemically defined reaction, rxn_006 should release H2O as the Strecker byproduct, and rxn_008 should consume 2 H2O and release NH3/NH4+ to conserve mass. Transfer from spark products into aqueous surface chemistry is plausible but should be encoded explicitly.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_006", "rxn_008"], "suggestions": ["Replace rxn_001 with separate balanced spark/plume product-generation reactions or a product-pool node plus explicit transfer to water", "Correct rxn_006 to include H2O as the Strecker byproduct", "Rewrite rxn_008 as mol_008 + 2 mol_003 -> mol_012 + mol_002 (or NH4+ equivalent) and specify hydrolysis conditions"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_001": 0.28, "rxn_007": 0.54, "rxn_008": 0.48}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.42, "pathway_coherence": 0.71, "environmental_progression": 0.58, "target_achievement": 0.61, "overall_confidence": 0.5, "feedback": "The ferrocyanide-buffered Strecker concept matches literature attempts to address HCN dilution, but the encoding omits the ferrocyanide or Prussian Blue reservoir, aerosol context, and the water byproduct of Strecker chemistry. rxn_001 remains an unbalanced pool reaction, rxn_007 is missing H2O formation, and rxn_008 is not ma