they should be split into separate competing reactions with catalyst, pH, and H2 conditions. rxn_012 likely needs added water and NH3 release after cyanide capture/hydrolysis. Also fix the mol_008 structure or InChI inconsistency and add reaction-level citations tying individual nodes to the cited studies. No pathway is approvable in its current formalization, even though the conceptual literature framing is good."}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 189784.9383354187, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T21:25:36.832350", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Valine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\nThe following deep research report contains peer-reviewed literature on prebiotic synthesis\nof Valine. Use it as evidence to inform your network design.\n\n=== DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of Valine: A Cross-Framework Evidence Review\n\nValine is one of the most robustly \"prebiotically accessible\" proteinogenic amino acids at the level of detection \u2014 it appears in spark-discharge products, carbonaceous chondrites, asteroid-return samples and interstellar-ice photolysis residues \u2014 but no single route yet delivers it selectively, at high yield, from simple feedstocks under one set of geochemically defended conditions. The best-characterized end-game steps are (i) Strecker chemistry on isobutyraldehyde and (ii) metal-catalyzed reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate (\u03b1-KIV); the critical bottleneck in every framework is the prebiotic origin of the branched C4/C5 skeleton (isobutyraldehyde or \u03b1-KIV), and the biosynthetic pyruvate \u2192 acetolactate \u2192 \u03b1-KIV sequence has no published nonenzymatic demonstration.\n\n## TL;DR\n\n- **Valine forms easily but non-selectively.** It has been confirmed in spark-discharge products (Ring et al. 1972, PNAS; Johnson et al. 2009, OLEB reanalysis of Miller's original samples), in Murchison and other CM chondrites, in Bennu samples (Glavin et al. 2025, Nature Astronomy), and in UV-irradiated interstellar ice analogues (Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. 2002, Nature) \u2014 always as a minor, racemic component accompanied by its isomers norvaline and isovaline.\n- **Two end-game chemistries are experimentally solid; the skeleton is not.** Isobutyraldehyde + HCN + NH3 (Strecker) and \u03b1-KIV + NH3 + H2 over Ni, Pd or ground iron meteorite (Kaur et al. 2024, Chem; 22 \u00b0C, pH 8, no reaction without catalyst) both give valine, but how isobutyraldehyde or \u03b1-KIV arise prebiotically is either demonstrated only in a multistep, operator-staged network (Patel et al. 2015, Nature Chemistry: acetone \u2192 valine aminonitrile via HCN/H2S/UV/Cu) or not demonstrated at all (nonenzymatic acetolactate pathway).\n- **All routes are contested.** Spark discharge depends on reducing atmospheres; cyanosulfidic chemistry on high sulfide, 254-nm UV and staged reagent delivery; metabolism-first routes on a supply of \u03b1-KIV that nobody has made abiotically; and extraterrestrial delivery supplies only nmol/g-level valine. For network design, treat valine as a downstream \"skeleton-limited\" target and prioritize finding a prebiotic source of the C5 branched skeleton.\n\n## Key Findings\n\n| Route / framework | Feedstocks | Key valine-relevant step | Valine actually detected/produced? | Catalyst | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Spark discharge (Miller-Urey and variants) | CH4, N2/NH3, H2O, H2 (also CO/CO2 + H2) | Radical gas-phase chemistry \u2192 aldehydes + HCN \u2192 Strecker in aqueous phase | Yes (Ring et al. 1972; Johnson et al. 2009 reanalysis) | None required | Contested (atmospheric redox state) |\n| Strecker synthesis on isobutyraldehyde | Isobutyraldehyde, HCN, NH3 | \u03b1-aminonitrile \u2192 hydrolysis | Yes (textbook; prebiotic variants incl. ferrocyanide/aerosol, Ruiz-Bermejo et al. 2013) | None required | End-game solid; aldehyde origin contested |\n| Cyanosulfidic network (Sutherland) | HCN, H2S/HS\u207b, phosphate, Cu, 254-nm UV | Acetone \u2192 cyanohydrin \u2192 reductive homologation \u2192 valine \u03b1-aminonitrile | Valine precursor (aminonitrile) shown; no yield stated | Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox, HS\u207b reductant | Contested (sulfide levels, UV, staging) |\n| Reductive amination of \u03b1-KIV | \u03b1-KIV, NH3/NH4\u207a, H2 (or formate) | Imine formation + hydrogenation | Yes (Kaur et al. 2024, Chem) | Ni/SiO2-Al2O3, Pd/C, Aletai iron meteorite; none = no reaction | End-game solid; \u03b1-KIV origin unsolved |\n| Bucherer\u2013Bergs on \u03b1-ketoacids | \u03b1-ketoacid, HCN, NH3, CO2 | Hydantoin \u2192 hydrolysis | General for \u03b1-ketoacids (Pulletikurti et al. 2022, Nature Chemistry) | None (bicarbonate buffer) | Requires \u03b1-KIV; cyanide relevance debated |\n| Biosynthetic analog (pyruvate \u2192 acetolactate \u2192 DHIV \u2192 \u03b1-KIV) | Pyruvate | ThDP condensation, reductoisomerization, dehydration | **No nonenzymatic demonstration** | \u2014 | Hypothetical |\n| Fischer\u2013Tropsch-type (FTT) | CO, H2, NH3 | Surface catalysis at 200\u2013500 \u00b0C | \"Several \u03b1-amino acids\" (Yoshino et al. 1971); not reproduced robustly | Fe\u2013Ni metal, magnetite | Contested |\n| HCN polymer hydrolysis | HCN | Oligomer hydrolysis | Not in standard HCN oligomers (Ferris et al. 1974); reported after \u2076\u2070Co \u03b3-irradiation of HCN | None | Weak for valine |\n| Interstellar ice UV photolysis | H2O, CH3OH, NH3, CO, CO2 (\u00b1HCN) | Radical chemistry at 12 K, hydrolysis of residue | Yes in Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. 2002; ee measured by Modica et al. 2014 | None (UV) | Accepted as possible source of meteoritic organics |\n| Impact shock | Comet-ice analogue | Shock heating | Not reported (glycine, alanine, AIB, isovaline only; Martins et al. 2013) | None | Not shown for valine |\n| Meteoritic/asteroidal delivery | Parent-body aqueous chemistry | Strecker/cyanohydrin in parent body | Yes, nmol/g level; racemic or contested L-excess | Parent-body minerals | Occurrence accepted; contribution debated |\n\n## Details\n\n### 1. Spark-discharge / Miller-Urey-type experiments\n\n**What was shown.** Ring, Wolman, Friedmann and Miller (1972, *PNAS* 69:765) ran electric discharges on CH4 + N2 + H2O with traces of NH3 and confirmed glycine, alanine, \u03b1-amino-n-butyric acid, \u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid, **valine, norvaline, isovaline**, leucine, isoleucine, alloisoleucine, norleucine, proline, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, serine, threonine and others.\\[1\\]\\[2\\] This is the classic demonstration that the hydrophobic branched amino acids form alongside their unbranched isomers \u2014 valine is produced together with norvaline and isovaline, so any analysis must resolve the C5 isomers chromatographically.\n\n**Archived-sample reanalyses.** After Stanley Miller's death in 2007, Bada and colleagues found his dried 1950s residues. Johnson et al. (2008, *Science* 322:404) showed that the \"volcanic\" apparatus (an aspirator injecting steam into the spark flask) gave 22 amino acids and five amines, more diverse than the classic apparatus. Johnson et al. (2009, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres* 39:240) reported that the original samples contain several aminobutyric acid isomers plus serine, homoserine, isoserine, isovaline, **valine**, phenylalanine, ornithine and others, and that both diversity and yield increased with the water-aspirating device. Parker et al. (2011, *PNAS*) analyzed an unpublished 1958 H2S-rich experiment (CH4, NH3, H2S, CO2), reporting the first sulfur amino acids from spark experiments and the highest relative yields of aminobutyric acid isomers ever found in a spark experiment; that paper's emphasis is on sulfur and C4 amino acids, and this review does not rely on it for a valine-specific yield.\\[3\\]\n\n**Conditions and yields.** Ambient temperature, 1 bar-scale gas mixtures, electrical energy; no catalyst. Schlesinger and Miller (1983, *Journal of Molecular Evolution*) showed that total amino acid yields are 1.2\u20134.7% (based on carbon) for CH4-containing mixtures largely independent of H2/CH4 ratio, about 2% for H2 + CO or H2 + CO2 mixtures at H2/C ratios of 2\u20134, but only ~10\u207b\u00b3% when H2/CO2 = 0.\\[4\\] Higher amino acids such as valine are a small fraction of the total.\n\n**Mechanism.** Discharge produces HCN, aldehydes and ketones in the gas phase; amino acids form in the aqueous trap by Strecker (aldehyde + HCN + NH3) and related cyanohydrin chemistry. Valine thus derives from isobutyraldehyde formed by gas-phase radical recombination.\n\n**Critiques.** The main objection is atmospheric redox state: if the Hadean atmosphere was CO2/N2-dominated, yields collapse (the 10\u207b\u00b3% figure above). Defenders argue that volcanic plumes (Johnson et al. 2008) or transiently H2-rich post-impact atmospheres provide local reducing conditions. A second critique is selectivity: valine is a minor product in a broad mixture with non-proteinogenic isomers.\n\n### 2. Strecker synthesis from isobutyraldehyde \u2014 and the origin of isobutyraldehyde\n\n**Chemistry.** Isobutyraldehyde (2-methylpropanal) + NH3 + HCN \u2192 2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile (valine \u03b1-aminonitrile) \u2192 hydrolysis \u2192 racemic valine. No catalyst is required; the reaction proceeds in water at ambient temperature, with the aminonitrile/cyanohydrin partition controlled by NH3/HCN ratios and pH. Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*) used aqueous aerosols with UV and ferrocyanide or Prussian Blue as the cyanide source to perform Strecker syntheses of alanine, valine and phenylalanine, presenting ferrocyanides as a way to store cyanide in an Fe(II)-bearing ocean.\n\n**Competing sinks for isobutyraldehyde.** Isobutyraldehyde is also the precursor of pantoic acid: Miller and Schlesinger (1993, *Journal of Molecular Evolution*) reported that pantoic acid forms in good prebiotic yield from isobutyraldehyde (or \u03b1-KIV) + H2CO + HCN. Fairchild et al. (2024, *Science*; Powner group) showed a phosphate-catalyzed, neutral-pH aldol of formaldehyde with isobutyraldehyde \u2014 the Strecker precursors of glycine and valine \u2014 selectively leading to pantolactone/pantetheine. This shows that, in a mixture rich in formaldehyde, isobutyraldehyde is partly diverted from valine toward coenzyme A precursors; network designers should model this branch explicitly.\n\n**Critiques.** Strecker chemistry itself is uncontroversial; the open question is the supply of isobutyraldehyde. Proposed sources include spark/plume chemistry (above), the cyanosulfidic homologation of acetone (below), FTT chemistry, and decarboxylation of \u03b1-KIV (which presupposes \u03b1-KIV). Dilute HCN is a further problem: Stribling and Miller (1987, *Origins of Life and Evolution of the Biosphere* 17:261) calculated, for an HCN production rate of 100 nmol cm\u207b\u00b2 yr\u207b\u00b9, a steady-state primitive-ocean HCN concentration of about 4 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2076 M at pH 8 and 0 \u00b0C, pushing Strecker chemistry toward evaporitic, eutectic-freezing or ferrocyanide-storage scenarios.\n\n### 3. Cyanosulfidic / systems-chemistry route (Sutherland group)\n\n**What was shown.** Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy and Sutherland (2015, *Nature Chemistry* 7:301) derived precursors of ribonucleotides, amino acids and lipids by reductive homologation of HCN and its derivatives. Valine is accessible: dihydroxyacetone reduction gives acetone; acetone + HCN gives a cyanohydrin; reaction with H2S gives an \u03b1-hydroxythioamide; Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox cycling under 254-nm UV with HS\u207b as stoichiometric reductant removes the hydroxyl to give the isobutyraldehyde-level product, which is trapped as a cyanohydrin and converted by NH3 to the **valine \u03b1-aminonitrile**. The authors demonstrated the conversion of the constitutionally related intermediate to the valine aminonitrile by addition of ammonia and noted that a further homologation cycle gives the leucine aminonitrile. The network accesses 12 amino acids via Strecker intermediates. A later paper (Liu et al. 2023, *Nature Chemistry*, \"Thiophosphate photochemistry enables prebiotic access to sugars and terpenoid precursors\") showed thiophosphate (PSO3\u00b3\u207b) photochemistry improves parts of this network and preserves access to valine and leucine.\n\n**Yields.** Patel et al. explicitly decline to give discrete yields for individual products, arguing that in a multi-product network the yield of any one compound is less meaningful; no percentage is published for the valine aminonitrile from acetone. Benchmark homologation steps in related Sutherland-group work (e.g., glycolaldehyde ~42% after 4 h with CuCN/H2S under UV) indicate individual steps can be efficient, but the cumulative yield of the multistep valine sequence is unknown.\n\n**Environment proposed.** Surface streams and pools on an early Earth with UV exposure, cyanide stored as ferrocyanide salts (released by impact/thermal processing), sulfide from volcanism or impacts, and \"confluence\" of streams delivering different intermediates.\n\n**Critiques.** (i) Sulfide availability: Ranjan, Todd, Sutherland and Sasselov (2018, *Astrobiology* 18:1023) concluded that volcanic outgassing \"could have supplied prebiotically relevant levels of SO2-derived anions, but not H2S-derived anions,\" and that \"epochs of moderately high volcanism could have been especially conducive to cyanosulfidic prebiotic chemistry,\" so the chemistry needs specific local settings (e.g., impact-supplied sulfide or high-outgassing epochs). (ii) The use of 254-nm UV and Cu catalysts, and stepwise reagent addition by the experimenter, is criticized as idealized relative to a natural setting. (iii) Congruence with biochemistry: the route reaches valine via aminonitriles, not via \u03b1-KIV and transamination as in extant metabolism. (iv) Comment literature notes the chemistry requires H2S from the outset, possibly corresponding to a later geochemical stage.\n\n### 4. Reductive amination and transamination of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate (metal/mineral catalysis)\n\n**Strongest valine-specific demonstration.** Kaur, Rauscher, Werner, Song, Yi, Kaz\u00f6ne, Martin, T\u00fcys\u00fcz and Moran (2024, *Chem* 10:1) showed that H2 drives nonenzymatic reductive amination of six biological \u03b1-ketoacids \u2014 pyruvate, \u03b1-ketoglutarate, oxaloacetate, \u03b1-ketoisocaproate, **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate**, \u03b1-keto-\u03b2-methylvalerate \u2014 and glyoxylate to glycine, alanine, glutamate, aspartate, leucine, **valine** and isoleucine.\\[5\\] Conditions: 30 mM ketoacid, 5 bar H2, NH4HCO3 (5 equiv, 150 mM), pH 8 water, 22 \u00b0C, 72 h, with Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 (10 mol% Ni), Pd/C (1 mol%) or 3 mg ground Aletai iron meteorite (18 mol% Ni). With Ni and meteorite, combined amino + hydroxy acid yields were 6\u201384% at roughly 1:1 amino:hydroxy acid; with Pd, 52\u201388% with >5:1 selectivity for the amino acid in almost all cases. No reduction occurred without a metal catalyst. For pyruvate, ammonia as low as 6 mM gave alanine in up to 17% (Ni) or 44% (Pd) yield relative to ammonia \u2014 62 to 250 times lower ammonia than earlier prebiotic reductive aminations (375 mM\u20131.5 M). The same conditions run H2-driven reductive aldol chemistry that builds \u03b1-ketoglutarate-family ketoacids, but not the branched \u03b1-KIV skeleton. The authors cite 1\u201315 mmol/kg dissolved H2 in hydrothermal fluids as a geological analog to their H2 pressures.\n\n**Earlier iron-sulfide and iron-hydroxide work.** Hafenbradl, Keller, W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user and Stetter (1995, *Tetrahedron Letters*) coupled reductive amination of \u03b1-oxo acids to oxidative pyrite formation (FeS/H2S).\\[6\\]\\[7\\] Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003, *Tetrahedron Letters* 44:1695) reported efficient reductive amination of \u03b1-keto acids with freshly precipitated FeS or Fe(OH)2 at pH near the amine pKa, using stoichiometric Fe(II) reductant and high ammonia.\\[8\\] Barge, Flores, Baum, VanderVelde and Russell (2019, *PNAS* 116:4828) showed mixed-valence iron oxyhydroxides (\"green rust\") convert pyruvate to alanine and lactate; alanine is favored at intermediate Fe redox state (~50% Fe(II)), at pH above the NH3 pKa (~9.25), and at 25\u201370 \u00b0C, with no alanine at pH 7.\\[9\\]\\[10\\]\\[11\\] Kitadai et al. (2019, *Science Advances*) electroreduced metal sulfides (e.g., FeS \u2192 Fe\u2070) at potentials expected at alkaline vent\u2013seawater interfaces and achieved >50% yields for reductive amination of four keto acids (1.25 mM each) in one pot. These studies did not focus on \u03b1-KIV; valine is expected by analogy but was not the reported target in the iron-mineral papers cited here.\n\n**Reactivity data.** Mayer and Moran (2022, *Angewandte Chemie*) quantified nonenzymatic reduction and reductive amination of keto acids with BH3CN\u207b as a model hydride, showing \u03b1-ketoglutarate is the least reactive Krebs ketoacid toward amination\\[12\\] \u2014 relevant for predicting partitioning between \u03b1-KIV and other ketoacids in mixtures.\n\n**Critiques.** (i) Substrate supply: every reductive-amination demonstration starts from purchased \u03b1-KIV; no abiotic \u03b1-KIV synthesis exists (see \u00a75). (ii) Competing carbonyl reduction yields \u03b1-hydroxyisovalerate in roughly equal amounts with Ni/meteorite catalysts. (iii) Earlier Fe(II)-mediated work required 375 mM\u20131.5 M NH3, considered implausibly high; Kaur et al. reduce this but require concentrated metal catalysts (or Pd, which is rare). (iv) Metal-catalyzed hydrogenation is a textbook industrial reaction, and critics question whether native Ni or platinum-group metals were locally concentrated; proponents point to awaruite (Ni3Fe) in serpentinites, metallic Ni particles in H2-rich vents and ~220 tonnes/year of Fe-Ni cosmic dust.\n\n### 5. Metabolism-first: the biosynthetic analogy (pyruvate \u2192 acetolactate \u2192 \u03b1-KIV \u2192 valine)\n\nIn biology, acetohydroxyacid synthase (ThDP-dependent) condenses two pyruvates to (S)-2-acetolactate + CO2; ketol-acid reductoisomerase (Mg\u00b2\u207a/NADPH) performs an alkyl migration plus reduction to 2,3-dihydroxyisovalerate; dihydroxyacid dehydratase ([Fe\u2013S] enzyme) gives \u03b1-KIV; and transamination yields valine.\\[13\\]\\[14\\]\n\n**Evidence status.** A targeted literature search found **no peer-reviewed nonenzymatic demonstration** of any of the three skeleton-building steps. Thiazolium salts catalyze benzoin/acyloin condensations of aldehydes (Sheehan and Hara 1974, *Journal of Organic Chemistry*), but no prebiotic, metal- or mineral-catalyzed pyruvate \u2192 acetolactate conversion has been reported. Acetolactate is known to undergo nonenzymatic oxidative decarboxylation to diacetyl/acetoin, i.e., abiotic chemistry tends to degrade rather than build this intermediate. The reductoisomerization couples methyl migration and reduction in a single active site, with intermediates not released, which makes a nonenzymatic analog mechanistically demanding.\n\n**Iron-promoted networks.** Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019, *Nature* 569:104) showed Fe\u00b2\u207a in 70 \u00b0C water drives a pyruvate + glyoxylate network that reproduces 9 of 11 Krebs-cycle intermediates and all five universal metabolic precursors, and with hydroxylamine and metallic iron gives four amino acids (glycine, alanine, aspartate, glutamate); valine is not among them. Stubbs et al. (2020, *Nature Chemistry*) and Krishnamurthy-group cyanide-reductant chemistry (2022, *Nature Chemistry*) build \u03b1-ketoacid networks from glyoxylate and pyruvate, also without the branched-chain pathway.\n\n**Alternative evolutionary hypothesis.** Keefe, Lazcano and Miller (1995, *Origins of Life and Evolution of the Biosphere*) argued the acetolactate pathway cannot be the primordial route because it has unstable intermediates, and proposed that branched-chain amino acids were first made by reductive carboxylation of short branched-chain fatty acids (isobutyric acid \u2192 \u03b1-KIV) followed by transamination. This is a proposal, not an experimental demonstration.\n\n**Thermodynamics.** Amend and Shock (1998, *Science* 281:1659) computed the net synthesis 5CO2(aq) + NH4\u207a + 12H2(aq) \u2192 valine(aq) + H\u207a + 8H2O. They report that \"\u0394Gr is negative for 11 of the 20 net amino acid synthesis reactions in a 100\u00b0C mixed hydrothermal solution,\" whereas \"all 20 amino acids are strongly endergonic in cooler (18\u00b0C), oxidized, surface seawater\"; the paper's valine table row (entries of \u221270.12 and 178.00) was not verified here as to which quantity each represents, so per-valine kJ/mol values should be taken from the primary table. This establishes thermodynamic drive in H2-rich settings but says nothing about kinetic pathways; Aubrey, Cleaves and Bada (2009, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*) cautioned that such equilibrium models neglect kinetics and degradation, observing A \u21cc B \u21cc C (precursor \u21cc amino acid \u21cc degradation product) behavior at 125\u2013175 \u00b0C.\n\n**Critiques.** The framework predicts congruence with extant biochemistry (\u03b1-KIV \u2192 valine by amination), which the Kaur et al. data support for the final step, but the absence of a nonenzymatic \u03b1-KIV synthesis is the single largest gap in the metabolism-first account of valine. High temperatures also accelerate amino acid decomposition.\n\n### 6. Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry on \u03b1-ketoacids\n\nPulletikurti, Yadav, Springsteen and Krishnamurthy (2022, *Nature Chemistry* 14:1142) showed \u03b1-ketoacids react with cyanide and ammonia in bicarbonate buffer at room temperature via the Bucherer\u2013Bergs pathway to hydantoins, which hydrolyze (80 \u00b0C) to \u03b1-amino acids; e.g., \u03b1-ketoglutarate gave the hydantoin in 96% yield, with slower hydrolysis to glutamate (19% with 72% hydantoin remaining). The route bridges cyanide chemistry and ketoacid metabolism. For valine it would require \u03b1-KIV and so inherits the same skeleton problem. Kaur et al. (2024) criticize cyanide-based routes as incongruent with metabolism, since cyanide is never directly used in biochemistry and is toxic to metalloenzymes.\n\n### 7. Fischer\u2013Tropsch-type (FTT) and CO/H2 surface chemistry\n\nYoshino, Hayatsu and Anders (1971, *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta* 35:927) reported several \u03b1-amino acids and \u03b2-alanine from CO + H2 + NH3 over Fe\u2013Ni catalysts at 200\u2013500 \u00b0C, proposed as a solar-nebula source of meteoritic organics. A NASA follow-up (NASA TM X-62,411, 1975) heating CO/H2/NH3 with iron meteorite found only small quantities of a few amino acids, \"at odds with the existing literature.\" Glavin et al. (2010, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*) and Elsila et al. (2021, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*) note that FTT synthesis needs re-examination with modern analytics. Valine specifically has not been robustly shown by FTT; the route is contested.\n\n### 8. HCN polymer hydrolysis, formamide and radiolysis\n\nFerris, Wos, Nooner and Or\u00f3 (1974, *Journal of Molecular Evolution* 3:225) identified glycine, aspartic acid and diaminosuccinic acid as major and alanine, isoleucine and \u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid as minor amino acids from hydrolyzed HCN oligomers \u2014 valine is not among them. Ferris et al. (1978, *Journal of Molecular Evolution* 11:293) showed 0.1 M HCN oligomerizes at pH 9.2. Sweeney, Toste and Ponnamperuma (1976, *Origins of Life* 7:187) exposed aqueous HCN solutions (0.004\u20130.1 M) to cobalt-60 gamma rays and identified glycine, alanine, valine, serine, threonine, aspartic acid and glutamic acid among the hydrolysis products; Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013, *Life*) list 12 of 20 protein amino acids across HCN-polymer studies. Valine is therefore a minor, condition-dependent product of HCN polymer chemistry rather than a characteristic one. Formamide-based chemistry has been developed mainly for nucleobases; this review found no valine-specific formamide synthesis.\n\n### 9. Interstellar ice irradiation\n\nMu\u00f1oz Caro et al. (2002, *Nature* 416:403) UV-irradiated H2O:CH3OH:NH3:CO:CO2 (2:1:1:1:1) ice at 12 K and identified 16 amino acids in the hydrolyzed room-temperature residue, valine among them. Bernstein et al. (2002, *Nature* 416:401) independently found glycine, alanine and serine from H2O/CH3OH/NH3/HCN ices. Modica et al. (2014, *Astrophysical Journal*) measured small enantiomeric excesses induced by circularly polarized UV for five amino acids including valine and norvaline. Critique: products are detected only after acid hydrolysis, so precursors rather than free valine form in the ice; yields are tiny; relevance depends on delivery and survival.\n\n### 10. Impact/shock synthesis\n\nMartins, Price, Goldman, Sephton and Burchell (2013, *Nature Geoscience* 6:1045) fired projectiles at ~7 km/s into comet-ice analogues and obtained racemic alanine, \u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid and isovaline (plus precursors) after hydrolysis. Valine was not reported. Impact routes are therefore unproven for valine.\n\n### 11. Meteoritic occurrence and extraterrestrial delivery\n\n**Abundance.** Valine is a minor meteoritic amino acid. Glavin et al. (2021, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*) reported amino acids in Aguas Zarcas and Murchison at ~0.1 to 158 nmol/g overall; valine was at the low end, too low for compound-specific \u03b4\u00b9\u00b3C measurement. Bennu samples (Glavin et al. 2025, *Nature Astronomy* 9:199) contain 33 amino acids including 14 of the 20 protein amino acids, valine among them, with chiral non-protein amino acids racemic or nearly so; the same paper measured an L-valine excess of ~34% in an acid-hydrolyzed hot-water extract but also observed elevated L-valine in the procedural blank, so laboratory contamination is a possible explanation.\n\n**Enantiomeric data \u2014 contested.** Murchison's D/L valine ratio of 0.67 \u00b1 0.03 (\u224820% L-excess) in Glavin et al. (2021) differs from Aguas Zarcas and Murchison soil and may be extraterrestrial, but could not be confirmed isotopically. Pizzarello and Cronin (2000, *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta*) found \u03b1-H amino acids including valine racemic or attributed excesses to contamination, with the documented meteoritic excesses confined to \u03b1-methyl amino acids such as isovaline (up to 18.5% L in Murchison; Glavin and Dworkin 2009, *PNAS*). Dworkin et al. (2026, arXiv:2609.12249), an OSIRIS-REx contamination-knowledge paper, report that \"the hummocky sample shows an excess of ~20% L-valine (Kim et al. 2026),\" conclude that this excess \"is not attributable to spacecraft contamination,\" and cite only a modest 2.2% L-valine excess as the sole earlier chondrite report (Pizzarello and Cronin 2000). These literatures conflict; any valine L-excess in meteorites should be regarded as unconfirmed. In the primitive CM Asuka 12236, valine D/L ratios of ~0.82\u20130.89 indicate near-racemic composition (Glavin et al. 2020, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*).\n\n**Isomer context.** Meteorites contain valine together with norvaline, isovaline and other C5 amino acids, consistent with Strecker/cyanohydrin chemistry on parent bodies acting on mixtures of C4 aldehydes and ketones (isobutyraldehyde \u2192 valine; butanal \u2192 norvaline; 2-butanone \u2192 isovaline). The dominance of \u03b1-methyl amino acids such as isovaline in some chondrites contrasts with biology's selection of valine.\n\n**Critiques.** Delivery fluxes and survival through atmospheric entry/impact limit concentrations; nmol/g valine in meteorites cannot by itself sustain concentration-dependent chemistry without strong local accumulation.\n\n## Environmental Context\n\n- **Reducing atmospheres/volcanic plumes with lightning**: supported for valine by spark experiments, contingent on local reducing gas compositions (Johnson et al. 2008).\n- **UV-lit surface pools/streams with HCN, sulfide, Cu and phosphate (cyanosulfidic)**: valine aminonitrile demonstrated; sulfide and UV requirements are the main plausibility constraint, with Ranjan et al. (2018, *Astrobiology*) finding outgassing could supply SO2-derived but not H2S-derived anions at relevant levels.\n- **H2-rich hydrothermal/serpentinizing systems with Ni/Fe-Ni alloys or iron oxyhydroxides**: best supported for the final \u03b1-KIV \u2192 valine step (Kaur et al. 2024; Barge et al. 2019 for analogous pyruvate chemistry) and thermodynamically favorable (Amend and Shock 1998), but with no demonstrated \u03b1-KIV source.\n- **Meteorite parent bodies and interstellar ices**: proven valine sources at trace levels.\n\nOn the evidence, no single environment is demonstrated to make valine from simple feedstocks selectively. The most experimentally complete feedstock-to-valine chain is the cyanosulfidic acetone route (multistep, staged), while the most mild, catalytic and biochemically congruent final step is metal-catalyzed reductive amination.\n\n## Open Questions and Challenges\n\n1. **The branched-skeleton problem.** No abiotic synthesis of \u03b1-KIV is published; isobutyraldehyde arises only from non-selective discharge/FTT chemistry or the staged cyanosulfidic homologation of acetone. This is the central bottleneck for valine.\n2. **Nonenzymatic acetolactate pathway.** Pyruvate \u2192 acetolactate, reductoisomerization and dehydration all lack nonenzymatic demonstrations; finding thiazolium-free, metal-promoted pyruvate homocoupling would be a high-value experiment.\n3. **Selectivity versus isomers.** Valine co-forms with norvaline and isovaline in all non-enzymatic chemistries; mechanisms that favor the branched \u03b1-H isomer are unknown.\n4. **Concentration and compatibility.** HCN dilution (~4 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2076 M at pH 8 and 0 \u00b0C in Stribling and Miller's 1987 primitive-ocean estimate), the ammonia requirement for amination, sulfide levels for photoredox chemistry, and isobutyraldehyde diversion to pantoate/pantetheine are all unresolved compatibility issues.\n5. **Congruence with biochemistry.** Strecker and cyanosulfidic routes do not pass through \u03b1-KIV, while metabolism-first routes match biology's final step but not its skeleton-building steps.\n6. **Chirality.** Abiotic valine is racemic; claimed meteoritic and Bennu L-excesses are contradictory and unconfirmed.\n\n## Recommendations for Synthesis-Network Design\n\n- Model two independent entry points into valine: (a) isobutyraldehyde + HCN + NH3 (catalyst-free Strecker), and (b) \u03b1-KIV + NH3 + H2 over Ni/Fe-Ni (or formate over Pd). Treat both as demonstrated end-games.\n- Treat the upstream skeleton step as the explicit unknown: include the cyanosulfidic acetone \u2192 cyanohydrin \u2192 reductive homologation branch (with no published yield) and flag the biosynthetic acetolactate branch as hypothetical.\n- Include the formaldehyde + isobutyraldehyde \u2192 pantolactone side reaction and \u03b1-hydroxyisovalerate formation as competing sinks.\n- Track norvaline and isovaline as co-products and use chromatographic separation of C5 isomers in any experimental validation.\n- Prioritize experiments on nonenzymatic pyruvate homocoupling or reductive carboxylation of isobutyrate (Keefe et al. 1995 hypothesis) over further optimization of the final amination step.\n\n## Caveats\n\n- Several valine-specific numbers (e.g., per-compound yields in Kaur et al. 2024 and absolute valine yields in spark experiments) are reported here only as ranges or qualitative detections because individual values were not extracted from primary tables.\n- The Amend and Shock per-valine free energies were not verified against the primary table and should be checked before quantitative use; the \u2076\u2070Co-HCN valine detection is from Sweeney, Toste and Ponnamperuma (1976, *Origins of Life*).\n- Absence of a nonenzymatic acetolactate-pathway demonstration is based on targeted searches; an obscure report could exist.\n\n## Sources\n\n1. [Prebiotic Synthesis of Hydrophobic and Protein Amino Acids](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.69.3.765)\n2. [ChemInform Abstract: New Insights into Prebiotic Chemistry from Stanley Miller\u2032s Spark Discharge Experiments](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/235337989_ChemInform_Abstract_New_Insights_into_Prebiotic_Chemistry_from_Stanley_Miller's_Spark_Discharge_Experiments)\n3. [(PDF) Abiotic synthesis of amino acids and self-crystallization under prebiotic conditions](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/267743575_Abiotic_synthesis_of_amino_acids_and_self-crystallization_under_prebiotic_conditions)\n4. [Prebiotic synthesis in atmospheres containing CH4, CO, and CO2](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02101642)\n5. [(PDF) A prebiotic Krebs cycle analog generates amino acids with H2 and NH3 over nickel](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/378533181_A_prebiotic_Krebs_cycle_analog_generates_amino_acids_with_H2_and_NH3_over_nickel)\n6. [Prebiotic Synthesis of N-Formylaminonitriles and Derivatives in Formamide](https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/jacs.2c13306)\n7. [Prebiotic synthesis of \u03b1-amino acids and orotate from \u03b1-ketoacids potentiates transition to extant metabolic pathways](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41557-022-00999-w)\n8. [Primordial reductive amination revisited - ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0040403902028630)\n9. [Laurie Barge, Ph.D. Senior Research Scientist, NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory](https://kiss.resources.caltech.edu/uploads/2026/09/etorganics-talk-2-barge.pdf)\n10. [Redox and pH gradients drive amino acid synthesis in iron oxyhydroxide mineral systems](https://www.pnas.org/doi/abs/10.1073/pnas.1812098116)\n11. [(PDF) Effects of Geochemical and Environmental Parameters on Abiotic Organic Chemistry Driven by Iron Hydroxide Minerals](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/346847223_Effects_of_Geochemical_and_Environmental_Parameters_on_Abiotic_Organic_Chemistry_Driven_by_Iron_Hydroxide_Minerals)\n12. [Reaction Mechanisms Quantifying Reductive Amination in Nonenzymatic Amino Acid](https://d-nb.info/1270928767/34)\n13. [Schematic of the branched-chain amino acid (BCAA) biosynthesis pathway...](https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Schematic-of-the-branched-chain-amino-acid-BCAA-biosynthesis-pathway-in-R-eutropha_fig1_264429993)\n14. [Biosynthesis of the Branched-Chain Amino Acids H. E. UMBARGER 27 INTRODUCTION](https://ecosalgenes.frenoy.eu/pdf/782.pdf)\n\n\nKey Citations:\n  1. Prebiotic Synthesis of Hydrophobic and Protein Amino Acids (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.69.3.765)\n  2. ChemInform Abstract: New Insights into Prebiotic Chemistry from Stanley Miller\u2032s Spark Discharge Experiments (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/235337989_ChemInform_Abstract_New_Insights_into_Prebiotic_Chemistry_from_Stanley_Miller's_Spark_Discharge_Experiments)\n  3. (PDF) Abiotic synthesis of amino acids and self-crystallization under prebiotic conditions (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/267743575_Abiotic_synthesis_of_amino_acids_and_self-crystallization_under_prebiotic_conditions)\n  4. Prebiotic synthesis in atmospheres containing CH4, CO, and CO2 (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02101642)\n  5. (PDF) A prebiotic Krebs cycle analog generates amino acids with H2 and NH3 over nickel (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/378533181_A_prebiotic_Krebs_cycle_analog_generates_amino_acids_with_H2_and_NH3_over_nickel)\n  6. Prebiotic Synthesis of N-Formylaminonitriles and Derivatives in Formamide (https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/jacs.2c13306)\n  7. Prebiotic synthesis of \u03b1-amino acids and orotate from \u03b1-ketoacids potentiates transition to extant metabolic pathways (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41557-022-00999-w)\n  8. Primordial reductive amination revisited - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0040403902028630)\n  9. Laurie Barge, Ph.D. Senior Research Scientist, NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory (https://kiss.resources.caltech.edu/uploads/2026/09/etorganics-talk-2-barge.pdf)\n  10. Redox and pH gradients drive amino acid synthesis in iron oxyhydroxide mineral systems (https://www.pnas.org/doi/abs/10.1073/pnas.1812098116)\n  11. (PDF) Effects of Geochemical and Environmental Parameters on Abiotic Organic Chemistry Driven by Iron Hydroxide Minerals (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/346847223_Effects_of_Geochemical_and_Environmental_Parameters_on_Abiotic_Organic_Chemistry_Driven_by_Iron_Hydroxide_Minerals)\n  12. Reaction Mechanisms Quantifying Reductive Amination in Nonenzymatic Amino Acid (https://d-nb.info/1270928767/34)\n  13. Schematic of the branched-chain amino acid (BCAA) biosynthesis pathway... (https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Schematic-of-the-branched-chain-amino-acid-BCAA-biosynthesis-pathway-in-R-eutropha_fig1_264429993)\n  14. Biosynthesis of the Branched-Chain Amino Acids H. E. UMBARGER 27 INTRODUCTION (https://ecosalgenes.frenoy.eu/pdf/782.pdf)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.43\n- Feedback: The network is connected, has sufficient size, and is well aligned with the literature at the strategic level: it correctly treats valine as skeleton-limited, includes the two best-supported end-games (Strecker from isobutyraldehyde and metal-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate), and explicitly retains the pantoate and alpha-hydroxyisovalerate side sinks plus the unresolved alpha-KIV bottleneck. However, as an encoded reaction network it is not valid enough for approval. The deterministic balance check finds 11 of 12 reactions unbalanced; only rxn_004 is balanced. That alone severely lowers network validity and chemical feasibility. The main repair priorities are: rxn_001-rxn_003 should be replaced by balanced spark/plume product-generation reactions or by a separate product-pool abstraction with explicit transfer, and the checker suggests H2 is likely among the coproducts if these are retained as overall stoichiometries; rxn_003 is also labeled sulfur-rich despite having no sulfur-containing reactant. rxn_006-rxn_007 should release H2O as Strecker byproduct. rxn_008 should be written as mol_008 + 2 mol_003 -> mol_012 + mol_002 (or NH4+ equivalent). rxn_005 should not be patched with the checker's generic O2/CO2 hints; instead it needs to be decomposed into the actual Patel-Sutherland substeps with HS-/H2S, Cu photoredox, UV, and staged delivery. rxn_009 is both unbalanced and unsupported by current literature and should be decomposed into explicit referenced steps or removed. rxn_010-rxn_011 incorrectly combine valine and alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid as co-products; they should be split into separate competing reactions with catalyst, pH, and H2 conditions. rxn_012 likely needs added water and NH3 release after cyanide capture/hydrolysis. Also fix the mol_008 structure or InChI inconsistency and add reaction-level citations tying individual nodes to the cited studies. No pathway is approvable in its current formalization, even though the conceptual literature framing is good.\n- Issues: ['Conceptually aligned with the classic Miller-to-Strecker route and with literature reporting valine in spark-discharge products. The pathway is weakened because rxn_001 is an unbalanced product-pool shorthand rather than a chemically defined reaction, rxn_006 should release H2O as the Strecker byproduct, and rxn_008 should consume 2 H2O and release NH3/NH4+ to conserve mass. Transfer from spark products into aqueous surface chemistry is plausible but should be encoded explicitly.', 'The ferrocyanide-buffered Strecker concept matches literature attempts to address HCN dilution, but the encoding omits the ferrocyanide or Prussian Blue reservoir, aerosol context, and the water byproduct of Strecker chemistry. rxn_001 remains an unbalanced pool reaction, rxn_007 is missing H2O formation, and rxn_008 is not mass-conserving as written.', 'This is conceptually close to the Patel-Sutherland feedstock-to-valine-aminonitrile sequence, and rxn_004 is the only balanced step in the entire network. However, rxn_005 collapses a staged HS-/H2S, Cu photoredox, UV-driven sequence into one unbalanced surface reaction, so the key chemistry is not faithfully represented; rxn_001 and rxn_008 also need correction.', 'Among the listed routes this is the best aligned with literature arguments for localized reducing volcanic-plume or aspirator settings feeding aqueous Strecker chemistry. Even so, rxn_002 is still an unbalanced pool-forming surrogate, rxn_006 should release H2O, and rxn_008 is under-specified and not mass-conserving as written.', 'This route combines a plausible localized spark context with a literature-backed ferrocyanide-assisted Strecker end-game, but the actual reaction encoding is incomplete. rxn_002 is not a balanced chemical transformation, rxn_007 lacks the explicit ferrocyanide/aerosol chemistry and the H2O byproduct, and rxn_008 again fails stoichiometric conservation.', 'The route captures the literature idea that acetone can feed a cyanosulfidic branch to valine aminonitrile, but the key transformation is too compressed to be reliable as encoded. rxn_005 omits the sulfur reagent, copper photoredox catalyst, UV requirement, and staging; rxn_002 is an unbalanced spark pool shorthand; rxn_008 is not stoichiometrically complete.', 'The Strecker end-game is chemically plausible, but the sulfur-rich spark initiation is poorly encoded. rxn_003 contains no sulfur species at all, so the stated environment and reactants are inconsistent; rxn_006 still needs H2O as byproduct, and rxn_008 remains under-specified for aminonitrile hydrolysis.', 'This combines two sulfur-dependent ideas, but neither is encoded faithfully. rxn_003 is labeled sulfur-rich with no sulfur reagent present, and rxn_005 omits HS-/H2S, Cu, UV, and staged delivery; only rxn_004 is balanced. The path remains conceptually recognizable from the literature but chemically underdefined as written.', 'The alpha-KIV-to-valine end-game is literature-supported, but the route fails at the skeleton step. rxn_009 is both unbalanced and unsupported by published nonenzymatic evidence, and the environment label Biochemical is not a defensible prebiotic setting; rxn_010 also incorrectly makes valine and alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid as stoichiometric co-products rather than alternative outcomes of reduction versus reductive amination.', 'Using Fe-Ni meteoritic material is geochemically imaginable and literature-backed for the final reductive amination step, but the pathway still depends on the same unsupported rxn_009. As written, rxn_011 also bundles valine formation and alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid formation into one impossible stoichiometric event rather than treating them as competing product channels.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Valine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Valine is treated as a skeleton-limited target. The network is built around the two experimentally strongest endgames: (i) Strecker capture of isobutyraldehyde to the valine aminonitrile followed by hydrolysis, and (ii) H2-driven reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate on Ni/Fe-Ni metal. Ten target-reaching pathways arise by combining two surface routes to isobutyraldehyde (reduced-gas discharge chemistry and the Patel acetone-based cyanosulfidic branch), two Strecker implementations (free HCN or ferrocyanide-released cyanide), one direct but speculative hydrothermal alpha-ketoisovalerate source, one speculative cyanide-hydrolysis bridge from isobutyraldehyde to alpha-ketoisovalerate, and two metal catalysts for the alpha-ketoacid endgame. The well-supported chemistry is concentrated in the endgames; the upstream branched-skeleton steps are kept explicit and flagged where speculative.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon monoxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon monoxide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfur\",\n      \"common_name\": \"elemental sulfur\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"propan-2-one\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetone\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetone cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9NOS\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanethioamide\",\n      \"common_name\": null,\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-methylpropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alpha-ketoisovaleric acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H11NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"valine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Spark/plume generation of acetone from reduced C1 gases\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"bulk 280-330K with transient discharge plasma hotspots\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approx. 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Reducing CH4/CO volcanic plume or atmospheric discharge; NH3 and H2O are commonly present in the full gas mixture but omitted from the net balance.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Electrical-discharge radical chemistry generates methyl and acyl fragments that recombine to a C3 carbonyl pool; acetone is represented as a condensed product.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"CH4-bearing spark-discharge systems generate broad carbonyl pools from which higher amino acids including valine emerge after aqueous cyanide/ammonia chemistry (Ring et al., 1972; Johnson et al., 2009). This node is a balanced, product-specific compression of that radical chemistry that assigns part of the discharge carbonyl pool to acetone so the experimentally demonstrated acetone-based Patel branch can be entered; the acetone assignment is therefore weaker than the downstream cyanosulfidic chemistry and depends on locally reducing plume conditions (Johnson et al., 2008; Schlesinger and Miller, 1983).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"HCN-bearing surface pool or stream, anoxic, neutral to mildly basic.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nucleophilic cyanide addition to the ketone forms the cyanohydrin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) explicitly used acetone as the branch-point ketone leading into valine precursor chemistry, and cyanohydrin formation from carbonyls and HCN is the standard entry to that network. Liu et al. (2023) supports continued prebiotic access to the same cyanide/sulfur photochemical regime.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrosulfide conversion of acetone cyanohydrin to hydroxythioamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"H2S/HS- rich surface water with staged sulfide delivery, anoxic.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The nitrile sulfurizes to the corresponding alpha-hydroxythioamide under sulfide-rich conditions.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) converted the acetone cyanohydrin into the corresponding alpha-hydroxythioamide as part of the reductive homologation sequence leading toward valine aminonitrile. This is one of the experimentally grounded substeps of the cyanosulfidic route, although the need for locally high sulfide remains a major geochemical constraint (Patel et al., 2015; Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu/UV sulfide-driven reduction of hydroxythioamide to isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox copper species\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox copper species\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Strong UV irradiation, anoxic, HS- supplies reducing equivalents, staged reagent delivery.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photoredox reduction removes the hydroxyl functionality from the alpha-hydroxythioamide and yields the higher aldehyde oxidation-state product; sulfide is oxidized to elemental sulfur.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The Cu/UV/HS- photoredox reduction of alpha-hydroxythioamides is the key Patel-Sutherland step that accesses the isobutyraldehyde oxidation level needed for valine precursor synthesis (Patel et al., 2015; Liu et al., 2023). The equation is written in sulfide-to-elemental-sulfur bookkeeping form to keep mass balance explicit. The chemistry is experimentally grounded, but its plausibility is limited to special surface settings with strong UV and unusually high local sulfide availability (Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Spark/plume generation of isobutyraldehyde from reduced C1 gases\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"bulk 280-330K with transient discharge plasma hotspots\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approx. 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Reducing CH4/CO volcanic plume or atmospheric discharge; condensed products later enter surface waters.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Radical gas-phase chain growth and carbonyl chemistry generate a branched C4 aldehyde pool that is then trapped in aqueous condensate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Valine is detected in classic spark-discharge products from reduced CH4-bearing gas mixtures, implying access to branched C4 carbonyl precursors before aqueous Strecker capture (Ring et al., 1972; Johnson et al., 2009). This node is therefore a balanced compression of discharge/plume product formation that assigns part of the carbonyl pool to isobutyraldehyde; that assignment is plausible but not directly isolated in the cited studies, and its global relevance depends on locally reducing atmospheres or volcanic plumes (Johnson et al., 2008; Schlesinger and Miller, 1983).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Classical Strecker addition to isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Neutral to mildly basic aqueous solution with coexisting NH3 and cyanide.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The aldehyde forms an imine with ammonia, cyanide adds to the iminium, and the alpha-aminonitrile is produced with water release.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Catalyst-free Strecker chemistry on isobutyraldehyde is one of the best-supported valine endgames in the literature. Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013) reported valine-forming Strecker chemistry in cyanide-bearing aerosol systems, consistent with the standard aldehyde + HCN + NH3 to aminonitrile sequence. The main uncertainty is upstream aldehyde supply, not this step itself; moreover, in formaldehyde-rich mixtures the same aldehyde can also be diverted toward pantoate/pantolactone chemistry rather than valine precursors (Miller and Schlesinger, 1993; Fairchild et al., 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Ferrocyanide-buffered aerosol Strecker addition to isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"ferrocyanide or Prussian Blue cyanide reservoir\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Aerosol droplets or evaporitic films; HCN input represents releasable cyanide equivalent from ferrocyanide/Prussian Blue under UV or thermal processing.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Released cyanide drives the same imine-then-cyanide-addition sequence as classical Strecker chemistry, but in cyanide-concentrating aerosols or evaporitic microenvironments.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013) demonstrated valine-forming Strecker chemistry in aqueous aerosols using ferrocyanide or Prussian Blue as cyanide sources. This variant is important because free primitive-ocean HCN may have been too dilute for bulk Strecker chemistry, making stored and then released cyanide a more defensible geochemical scenario (Stribling and Miller, 1987; Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Geothermal hydrolysis of valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Geothermal heating, prolonged aqueous residence, or wet-dry cycling.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential nitrile hydration and hydrolysis convert the alpha-aminonitrile into the amino acid with ammonia release.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydrolysis of alpha-aminonitriles is the established completion of Strecker chemistry. Amino-acid release only after hydrolysis is also characteristic of interstellar-ice residue studies, showing that nitrile-like precursors can persist until later aqueous heating (Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al., 2002). In a prebiotic surface setting, repeated heating and hydration can complete the same conversion (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Speculative FTT-like direct alpha-ketoisovalerate synthesis from CO and H2\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe-Ni alloy\", \"magnetite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"420-520K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe-Ni alloy / magnetite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"CO- and H2-rich subseafloor hydrothermal pores, anoxic.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface CO hydrogenation and C-C coupling on Fe-Ni/magnetite are taken to generate a branched C5 oxygenate pool, with alpha-ketoisovalerate represented as the target-specific alpha-ketoacid product.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Direct abiotic synthesis of alpha-ketoisovalerate has not been demonstrated; this step is therefore explicitly speculative. It extrapolates contested Fischer-Tropsch-type CO/H2 surface chemistry on Fe-Ni/magnetite, which has been reported to generate amino acids and related organics, to the specific branched alpha-ketoacid needed for valine (Yoshino et al., 1971; Glavin et al., 2010; Elsila et al., 2021). It is included because the review identifies alpha-ketoisovalerate supply as the central unresolved bottleneck, while H2-rich hydrothermal settings remain thermodynamically favorable for amino-acid synthesis overall (Amend and Shock, 1998).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Speculative cyanide-hydrolysis bridge from isobutyraldehyde to alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"mixed-valence Fe minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"mixed-valence Fe minerals\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Geothermal mineral interface; net step compresses cyanohydrin formation, hydrolysis to an alpha-hydroxy acid, and dehydrogenation to the alpha-ketoacid.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Isobutyraldehyde first equilibrates with its cyanohydrin, nitrile hydrolysis yields the corresponding alpha-hydroxy acid, and mineral-mediated dehydrogenation gives alpha-ketoisovalerate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"No paper in the provided set directly converts isobutyraldehyde to alpha-ketoisovalerate. This reaction is therefore speculative and represents a compressed sequence of cyanohydrin formation/hydrolysis to an alpha-hydroxy acid followed by mineral-mediated dehydrogenation to the alpha-ketoacid. The proposal is chemically motivated by the well-established cyanohydrin chemistry of isobutyraldehyde (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013), the ketoacid/hydroxyacid redox pairing seen in nonenzymatic amino-acid synthesis from alpha-ketoacids (Kaur et al., 2024), and broader Fe-enabled ketoacid interconversion chemistry (Muchowska et al., 2019). It is included as a testable crossover between cyanide-first and ketoacid-first valine routes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Ni-rich metal reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni-rich metal surface (awaruite analog)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"moderate H2-rich to high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni-rich metal surface\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH around 8, NH4+/NH3 present, anoxic; hydrothermal pore or vent-wall mineral setting.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The alpha-ketoacid forms an imine with ammonia and is then hydrogenated on a Ni-rich metal surface to the amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) demonstrated H2-driven reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine at pH 8 and 22 C with Ni catalysts, with no reaction in the absence of metal. Here a geochemically plausible Ni-bearing analog to the laboratory catalyst is used. This is one of the strongest valine-specific endgames in the literature, although competing alpha-hydroxyisovalerate formation shows that the step is productive but not perfectly selective (Kaur et al., 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe-Ni meteoritic-metal reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe-Ni meteoritic metal\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"moderate H2-rich to high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe-Ni meteoritic metal\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH around 8, NH4+/NH3 present, anoxic; hydrothermal or H2-rich aqueous mineral setting.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"An imine formed from the alpha-ketoacid and ammonia is hydrogenated on Fe-Ni meteoritic metal to give valine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) also obtained valine from alpha-ketoisovalerate using ground Aletai iron meteorite, showing that Fe-Ni meteoritic metal can catalyze the same endgame. This makes meteoritic Fe-Ni grains or analogous native Fe-Ni metal a plausible prebiotic catalyst class, though the local availability of sufficiently reactive concentrated metal remains debated (Kaur et al., 2024).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone cyanosulfidic branch plus classical Strecker\",\n      \"description\": \"Reduced-gas discharge chemistry furnishes acetone, which enters the Patel-style cyanosulfidic branch to isobutyraldehyde, then classical Strecker capture and hydrolysis yield valine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone cyanosulfidic branch plus ferrocyanide Strecker\",\n      \"description\": \"The same acetone-to-isobutyraldehyde surface branch is coupled to a ferrocyanide-buffered aerosol Strecker implementation before aminonitrile hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct isobutyraldehyde source plus classical Strecker\",\n      \"description\": \"A reduced-gas discharge/plume branch generates isobutyraldehyde directly, which then undergoes classical Strecker chemistry and hydrolysis to valine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct isobutyraldehyde source plus ferrocyanide Strecker\",\n      \"description\": \"Direct isobutyraldehyde formation is followed by ferrocyanide-mediated cyanide release, Strecker capture, and aminonitrile hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct alpha-ketoisovalerate source plus Ni amination\",\n      \"description\": \"A speculative hydrothermal direct source of alpha-ketoisovalerate is completed by the experimentally supported Ni-catalyzed reductive amination endgame.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct alpha-ketoisovalerate source plus meteoritic amination\",\n      \"description\": \"The same speculative direct alpha-ketoisovalerate source is completed by Fe-Ni meteoritic-metal reductive amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone cyanosulfidic branch diverted into alpha-ketoisovalerate then Ni amination\",\n      \"description\": \"The Patel-style aldehyde branch is redirected by a speculative cyanide-hydrolysis bridge to alpha-ketoisovalerate, then closed by Ni-catalyzed reductive amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone cyanosulfidic branch diverted into alpha-ketoisovalerate then meteoritic amination\",\n      \"description\": \"The same speculative aldehyde-to-ketoacid bridge is followed by Fe-Ni meteoritic-metal reductive amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct isobutyraldehyde source diverted into alpha-ketoisovalerate then Ni amination\",\n      \"description\": \"A direct reduced-gas isobutyraldehyde source is converted by the speculative cyanide-hydrolysis bridge to alpha-ketoisovalerate, then aminated on Ni-rich metal.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct isobutyraldehyde source diverted into alpha-ketoisovalerate then meteoritic amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Direct isobutyraldehyde formation is followed by the speculative aldehyde-to-ketoacid bridge and Fe-Ni meteoritic-metal reductive amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_014\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Valine", "overall_strategy": "Valine is treated as a skeleton-limited target. The network is built around the two experimentally strongest endgames: (i) Strecker capture of isobutyraldehyde to the valine aminonitrile followed by hydrolysis, and (ii) H2-driven reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate on Ni/Fe-Ni metal. Ten target-reaching pathways arise by combining two surface routes to isobutyraldehyde (reduced-gas discharge chemistry and the Patel acetone-based cyanosulfidic branch), two Strecker implementations (free HCN or ferrocyanide-released cyanide), one direct but speculative hydrothermal alpha-ketoisovalerate source, one speculative cyanide-hydrolysis bridge from isobutyraldehyde to alpha-ketoisovalerate, and two metal catalysts for the alpha-ketoacid endgame. The well-supported chemistry is concentrated in the endgames; the upstream branched-skeleton steps are kept explicit and flagged where speculative.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CH4", "iupac_name": "methane", "common_name": "methane", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CO", "iupac_name": "carbon monoxide", "common_name": "carbon monoxide", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyan