e": "target", "environment_formed": null}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Spark/plume generation of acetone from reduced C1 gases", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "bulk 280-330K with transient discharge plasma hotspots", "pressure": "approx. 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": false, "other": "Reducing CH4/CO volcanic plume or atmospheric discharge; NH3 and H2O are commonly present in the full gas mixture but omitted from the net balance."}, "mechanism": "Electrical-discharge radical chemistry generates methyl and acyl fragments that recombine to a C3 carbonyl pool; acetone is represented as a condensed product.", "reasoning": "CH4-bearing spark-discharge systems generate broad carbonyl pools from which higher amino acids including valine emerge after aqueous cyanide/ammonia chemistry (Ring et al., 1972; Johnson et al., 2009). This node is a balanced, product-specific compression of that radical chemistry that assigns part of the discharge carbonyl pool to acetone so the experimentally demonstrated acetone-based Patel branch can be entered; the acetone assignment is therefore weaker than the downstream cyanosulfidic chemistry and depends on locally reducing plume conditions (Johnson et al., 2008; Schlesinger and Miller, 1983)."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Acetone cyanohydrin formation", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "HCN-bearing surface pool or stream, anoxic, neutral to mildly basic."}, "mechanism": "Nucleophilic cyanide addition to the ketone forms the cyanohydrin.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) explicitly used acetone as the branch-point ketone leading into valine precursor chemistry, and cyanohydrin formation from carbonyls and HCN is the standard entry to that network. Liu et al. (2023) supports continued prebiotic access to the same cyanide/sulfur photochemical regime."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Hydrosulfide conversion of acetone cyanohydrin to hydroxythioamide", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "H2S/HS- rich surface water with staged sulfide delivery, anoxic."}, "mechanism": "The nitrile sulfurizes to the corresponding alpha-hydroxythioamide under sulfide-rich conditions.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) converted the acetone cyanohydrin into the corresponding alpha-hydroxythioamide as part of the reductive homologation sequence leading toward valine aminonitrile. This is one of the experimentally grounded substeps of the cyanosulfidic route, although the need for locally high sulfide remains a major geochemical constraint (Patel et al., 2015; Ranjan et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Cu/UV sulfide-driven reduction of hydroxythioamide to isobutyraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_005", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox copper species"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox copper species", "aqueous": true, "other": "Strong UV irradiation, anoxic, HS- supplies reducing equivalents, staged reagent delivery."}, "mechanism": "Photoredox reduction removes the hydroxyl functionality from the alpha-hydroxythioamide and yields the higher aldehyde oxidation-state product; sulfide is oxidized to elemental sulfur.", "reasoning": "The Cu/UV/HS- photoredox reduction of alpha-hydroxythioamides is the key Patel-Sutherland step that accesses the isobutyraldehyde oxidation level needed for valine precursor synthesis (Patel et al., 2015; Liu et al., 2023). The equation is written in sulfide-to-elemental-sulfur bookkeeping form to keep mass balance explicit. The chemistry is experimentally grounded, but its plausibility is limited to special surface settings with strong UV and unusually high local sulfide availability (Ranjan et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Spark/plume generation of isobutyraldehyde from reduced C1 gases", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "bulk 280-330K with transient discharge plasma hotspots", "pressure": "approx. 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": false, "other": "Reducing CH4/CO volcanic plume or atmospheric discharge; condensed products later enter surface waters."}, "mechanism": "Radical gas-phase chain growth and carbonyl chemistry generate a branched C4 aldehyde pool that is then trapped in aqueous condensate.", "reasoning": "Valine is detected in classic spark-discharge products from reduced CH4-bearing gas mixtures, implying access to branched C4 carbonyl precursors before aqueous Strecker capture (Ring et al., 1972; Johnson et al., 2009). This node is therefore a balanced compression of discharge/plume product formation that assigns part of the carbonyl pool to isobutyraldehyde; that assignment is plausible but not directly isolated in the cited studies, and its global relevance depends on locally reducing atmospheres or volcanic plumes (Johnson et al., 2008; Schlesinger and Miller, 1983)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Classical Strecker addition to isobutyraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_004", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Neutral to mildly basic aqueous solution with coexisting NH3 and cyanide."}, "mechanism": "The aldehyde forms an imine with ammonia, cyanide adds to the iminium, and the alpha-aminonitrile is produced with water release.", "reasoning": "Catalyst-free Strecker chemistry on isobutyraldehyde is one of the best-supported valine endgames in the literature. Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013) reported valine-forming Strecker chemistry in cyanide-bearing aerosol systems, consistent with the standard aldehyde + HCN + NH3 to aminonitrile sequence. The main uncertainty is upstream aldehyde supply, not this step itself; moreover, in formaldehyde-rich mixtures the same aldehyde can also be diverted toward pantoate/pantolactone chemistry rather than valine precursors (Miller and Schlesinger, 1993; Fairchild et al., 2024)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Ferrocyanide-buffered aerosol Strecker addition to isobutyraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_004", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["ferrocyanide or Prussian Blue cyanide reservoir"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Aerosol droplets or evaporitic films; HCN input represents releasable cyanide equivalent from ferrocyanide/Prussian Blue under UV or thermal processing."}, "mechanism": "Released cyanide drives the same imine-then-cyanide-addition sequence as classical Strecker chemistry, but in cyanide-concentrating aerosols or evaporitic microenvironments.", "reasoning": "Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013) demonstrated valine-forming Strecker chemistry in aqueous aerosols using ferrocyanide or Prussian Blue as cyanide sources. This variant is important because free primitive-ocean HCN may have been too dilute for bulk Strecker chemistry, making stored and then released cyanide a more defensible geochemical scenario (Stribling and Miller, 1987; Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013)."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Geothermal hydrolysis of valine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Geothermal heating, prolonged aqueous residence, or wet-dry cycling."}, "mechanism": "Sequential nitrile hydration and hydrolysis convert the alpha-aminonitrile into the amino acid with ammonia release.", "reasoning": "Hydrolysis of alpha-aminonitriles is the established completion of Strecker chemistry. Amino-acid release only after hydrolysis is also characteristic of interstellar-ice residue studies, showing that nitrile-like precursors can persist until later aqueous heating (Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al., 2002). In a prebiotic surface setting, repeated heating and hydration can complete the same conversion (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013)."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Speculative FTT-like direct alpha-ketoisovalerate synthesis from CO and H2", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_007"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Fe-Ni alloy", "magnetite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "420-520K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "Fe-Ni alloy / magnetite", "aqueous": true, "other": "CO- and H2-rich subseafloor hydrothermal pores, anoxic."}, "mechanism": "Surface CO hydrogenation and C-C coupling on Fe-Ni/magnetite are taken to generate a branched C5 oxygenate pool, with alpha-ketoisovalerate represented as the target-specific alpha-ketoacid product.", "reasoning": "Direct abiotic synthesis of alpha-ketoisovalerate has not been demonstrated; this step is therefore explicitly speculative. It extrapolates contested Fischer-Tropsch-type CO/H2 surface chemistry on Fe-Ni/magnetite, which has been reported to generate amino acids and related organics, to the specific branched alpha-ketoacid needed for valine (Yoshino et al., 1971; Glavin et al., 2010; Elsila et al., 2021). It is included because the review identifies alpha-ketoisovalerate supply as the central unresolved bottleneck, while H2-rich hydrothermal settings remain thermodynamically favorable for amino-acid synthesis overall (Amend and Shock, 1998)."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Speculative cyanide-hydrolysis bridge from isobutyraldehyde to alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_004", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["mixed-valence Fe minerals"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "mixed-valence Fe minerals", "aqueous": true, "other": "Geothermal mineral interface; net step compresses cyanohydrin formation, hydrolysis to an alpha-hydroxy acid, and dehydrogenation to the alpha-ketoacid."}, "mechanism": "Isobutyraldehyde first equilibrates with its cyanohydrin, nitrile hydrolysis yields the corresponding alpha-hydroxy acid, and mineral-mediated dehydrogenation gives alpha-ketoisovalerate.", "reasoning": "No paper in the provided set directly converts isobutyraldehyde to alpha-ketoisovalerate. This reaction is therefore speculative and represents a compressed sequence of cyanohydrin formation/hydrolysis to an alpha-hydroxy acid followed by mineral-mediated dehydrogenation to the alpha-ketoacid. The proposal is chemically motivated by the well-established cyanohydrin chemistry of isobutyraldehyde (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013), the ketoacid/hydroxyacid redox pairing seen in nonenzymatic amino-acid synthesis from alpha-ketoacids (Kaur et al., 2024), and broader Fe-enabled ketoacid interconversion chemistry (Muchowska et al., 2019). It is included as a testable crossover between cyanide-first and ketoacid-first valine routes."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Ni-rich metal reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_007"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Ni-rich metal surface (awaruite analog)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295-330K", "pressure": "moderate H2-rich to high", "catalyst": "Ni-rich metal surface", "aqueous": true, "other": "pH around 8, NH4+/NH3 present, anoxic; hydrothermal pore or vent-wall mineral setting."}, "mechanism": "The alpha-ketoacid forms an imine with ammonia and is then hydrogenated on a Ni-rich metal surface to the amino acid.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) demonstrated H2-driven reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine at pH 8 and 22 C with Ni catalysts, with no reaction in the absence of metal. Here a geochemically plausible Ni-bearing analog to the laboratory catalyst is used. This is one of the strongest valine-specific endgames in the literature, although competing alpha-hydroxyisovalerate formation shows that the step is productive but not perfectly selective (Kaur et al., 2024)."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Fe-Ni meteoritic-metal reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_007"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Fe-Ni meteoritic metal"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295-330K", "pressure": "moderate H2-rich to high", "catalyst": "Fe-Ni meteoritic metal", "aqueous": true, "other": "pH around 8, NH4+/NH3 present, anoxic; hydrothermal or H2-rich aqueous mineral setting."}, "mechanism": "An imine formed from the alpha-ketoacid and ammonia is hydrogenated on Fe-Ni meteoritic metal to give valine.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) also obtained valine from alpha-ketoisovalerate using ground Aletai iron meteorite, showing that Fe-Ni meteoritic metal can catalyze the same endgame. This makes meteoritic Fe-Ni grains or analogous native Fe-Ni metal a plausible prebiotic catalyst class, though the local availability of sufficiently reactive concentrated metal remains debated (Kaur et al., 2024)."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Acetone cyanosulfidic branch plus classical Strecker", "description": "Reduced-gas discharge chemistry furnishes acetone, which enters the Patel-style cyanosulfidic branch to isobutyraldehyde, then classical Strecker capture and hydrolysis yield valine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_006", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Acetone cyanosulfidic branch plus ferrocyanide Strecker", "description": "The same acetone-to-isobutyraldehyde surface branch is coupled to a ferrocyanide-buffered aerosol Strecker implementation before aminonitrile hydrolysis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Direct isobutyraldehyde source plus classical Strecker", "description": "A reduced-gas discharge/plume branch generates isobutyraldehyde directly, which then undergoes classical Strecker chemistry and hydrolysis to valine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Direct isobutyraldehyde source plus ferrocyanide Strecker", "description": "Direct isobutyraldehyde formation is followed by ferrocyanide-mediated cyanide release, Strecker capture, and aminonitrile hydrolysis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_005", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Direct alpha-ketoisovalerate source plus Ni amination", "description": "A speculative hydrothermal direct source of alpha-ketoisovalerate is completed by the experimentally supported Ni-catalyzed reductive amination endgame.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_009", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Direct alpha-ketoisovalerate source plus meteoritic amination", "description": "The same speculative direct alpha-ketoisovalerate source is completed by Fe-Ni meteoritic-metal reductive amination.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_009", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Acetone cyanosulfidic branch diverted into alpha-ketoisovalerate then Ni amination", "description": "The Patel