ified and not mass-conserving as written.', 'This route combines a plausible localized spark context with a literature-backed ferrocyanide-assisted Strecker end-game, but the actual reaction encoding is incomplete. rxn_002 is not a balanced chemical transformation, rxn_007 lacks the explicit ferrocyanide/aerosol chemistry and the H2O byproduct, and rxn_008 again fails stoichiometric conservation.', 'The route captures the literature idea that acetone can feed a cyanosulfidic branch to valine aminonitrile, but the key transformation is too compressed to be reliable as encoded. rxn_005 omits the sulfur reagent, copper photoredox catalyst, UV requirement, and staging; rxn_002 is an unbalanced spark pool shorthand; rxn_008 is not stoichiometrically complete.', 'The Strecker end-game is chemically plausible, but the sulfur-rich spark initiation is poorly encoded. rxn_003 contains no sulfur species at all, so the stated environment and reactants are inconsistent; rxn_006 still needs H2O as byproduct, and rxn_008 remains under-specified for aminonitrile hydrolysis.', 'This combines two sulfur-dependent ideas, but neither is encoded faithfully. rxn_003 is labeled sulfur-rich with no sulfur reagent present, and rxn_005 omits HS-/H2S, Cu, UV, and staged delivery; only rxn_004 is balanced. The path remains conceptually recognizable from the literature but chemically underdefined as written.', 'The alpha-KIV-to-valine end-game is literature-supported, but the route fails at the skeleton step. rxn_009 is both unbalanced and unsupported by published nonenzymatic evidence, and the environment label Biochemical is not a defensible prebiotic setting; rxn_010 also incorrectly makes valine and alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid as stoichiometric co-products rather than alternative outcomes of reduction versus reductive amination.', 'Using Fe-Ni meteoritic material is geochemically imaginable and literature-backed for the final reductive amination step, but the pathway still depends on the same unsupported rxn_009. As written, rxn_011 also bundles valine formation and alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid formation into one impossible stoichiometric event rather than treating them as competing product channels.']\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.77\n- Feedback: Network validity is good: the graph is connected, molecule IDs are used consistently, and the RDKit check reports 0 UNBALANCED reactions. However, rxn_001, rxn_004, rxn_005, rxn_008, rxn_009, and rxn_010 should be given explicit stoichiometric coefficients. Literature alignment is strong in its framing: the network correctly treats valine as skeleton-limited and centers the two best-supported endgames, Strecker capture of isobutyraldehyde (rxn_006/rxn_007 plus rxn_008) and metal-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate (rxn_011/rxn_012). The main weakness is exactly the bottleneck highlighted in the literature: branched-skeleton supply. rxn_005 is an overcompressed and nonselective discharge/plume summary, rxn_009 has no convincing experimental support as a direct abiotic alpha-ketoisovalerate synthesis, and rxn_010 lacks a defended oxidation or dehydrogenation mechanism and published precedent. Environmental consistency is moderate because the coarse Surface label collapses plume, UV-lit sulfide pool, and geothermal settings; rxn_004 and rxn_007 omit key condition-defining catalysts or cyanide-reservoir species; rxn_007 is stoichiometrically just a duplicate of rxn_006 as encoded; and P7-P10 do not justify surface-to-hydrothermal transfer. Best encoded pathway: P1. Overall, this is a literature-aware and connected exploratory network, but it is not approval-ready until the precursor-skeleton chemistry and environmental handoffs are made much more explicit.\n- Issues: ['Close to P1, but the ferrocyanide-buffered Strecker variant is only partially encoded because rxn_007 has the same net stoichiometry as rxn_006 and omits the ferrocyanide storage/release chemistry emphasized by Ruiz-Bermejo et al. The rest of the route is plausible but still inherits the discharge-source and cyanosulfidic staging constraints.', 'A coherent Miller/Strecker-style route. rxn_006 and rxn_008 are strong, but rxn_005 compresses a broad and nonselective discharge chemistry into a single isobutyraldehyde-forming step without representing competing C4 aldehydes, isomeric products, or the reducing-atmosphere caveat from the spark literature.', 'Same strengths as P3 with the added benefit of a literature-supported ferrocyanide-buffered Strecker endgame, but rxn_007 does not explicitly model ferrocyanide storage or cyanide release. The limiting issue remains rxn_005, the poorly constrained direct origin of isobutyraldehyde from reduced C1 gases.', 'The Ni-catalyzed reductive amination endgame is strongly aligned with Kaur et al. 2024, but the pathway relies on rxn_009, a direct abiotic alpha-ketoisovalerate synthesis that the supplied literature explicitly treats as unsolved. As encoded, the route reaches valine only by assuming the hardest step.', 'Similar to P5, but with a realistic Fe-Ni meteoritic catalyst for the final amination. The bottleneck is unchanged: rxn_009 has little experimental support, so the pathway remains hypothesis-driven despite a strong final step.', 'The acetone-to-isobutyraldehyde branch is the better supported part of this route, but diversion to alpha-ketoisovalerate through rxn_010 is very weak. That bridge lacks an explicit oxidation mechanism or literature precedent, and the later move from surface photochemistry to hydrothermal Ni catalysis is not physically justified in the network.', 'Same logic as P7 with a meteoritic metal endgame. The final amination is plausible, but rxn_010 remains the dominant weakness and the surface-to-hydrothermal transfer is still unspecified.', 'This route combines two under-supported skeleton-forming steps before a strong final amination. rxn_005 is an overcompressed discharge source of isobutyraldehyde, and rxn_010 is an unvalidated bridge to alpha-ketoisovalerate, so most of the uncertainty lies upstream of the otherwise sound Ni step.', 'Same upstream weaknesses as P9 with a plausible Fe-Ni meteoritic amination endgame. Because both precursor-forming steps are speculative, the final catalyst choice cannot rescue the overall path.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Valine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Valine is treated as a skeleton-limited target. The network therefore centers the two experimentally strongest late-stage solutions: classical Strecker chemistry on isobutyraldehyde and direct conversion of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid by mineral/metal-mediated reductive amination or by Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin chemistry. Upstream branched-skeleton supply is kept explicit and evidence-weighted: a surface acetone-to-isobutyraldehyde cyanosulfidic sequence is included because Patel-type chemistry reaches the valine aminonitrile branch, while a CO/H2 Fe-Ni route is retained only as a flagged hydrothermal alternative. Alpha-ketoisovaleric acid itself is left as a supplied hub intermediate because the literature identifies its abiotic synthesis as unresolved. Known competing sinks to pantoate and alpha-hydroxyisovalerate are acknowledged in the literature but not expanded here so the network remains within the requested size cap.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon monoxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon monoxide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\