  \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxo-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alpha-ketoisovaleric acid (alpha-KIV)\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H10N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-(propan-2-yl)imidazolidine-2,4-dione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"valine hydantoin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfur\",\n      \"common_name\": \"elemental sulfur\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H11NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"valine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"cyanide-rich evaporitic or geothermal pool; cyanohydrin capture favored during concentration and limited dilution\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds nucleophilically to the ketone carbonyl and the alkoxide is protonated to give the tertiary cyanohydrin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the entry step of the Patel/Sutherland branched-valine branch and is straightforward cyanohydrin chemistry under cyanide-bearing surface conditions (Patel et al., 2015; Liu et al., 2023). It is retained because it is one of the few experimentally grounded ways to begin assembling the branched valine skeleton from simple carbonyl chemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic conversion of acetone cyanohydrin to isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox pair\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II) salts\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"short-wave UV irradiation; HS-/H2S as stoichiometric reductant; staged delivery in shallow pools or streams\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The cyanohydrin is sulfurized through thioamide-like intermediates and then photoredox-driven deoxygenation and rearrangement deliver the aldehyde oxidation state.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. showed the acetone branch can be carried through sulfur chemistry and Cu photoredox to the valine aminonitrile level. This node is a mass-balanced summary of that staged sequence, keeping the required sulfide and UV explicit. The branch is experimentally important but geochemically contested because it needs localized sulfide, UV, and reagent staging (Patel et al., 2015; Liu et al., 2023; Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"FTT-like CO hydrogenation to isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe-Ni alloy\", \"magnetite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"450-550K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe-Ni alloy/magnetite\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"false\",\n        \"other\": \"subseafloor gas-water-mineral interface; rapid quenching needed to preserve aldehydes\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface-mediated carbonyl coupling and hydrogenation on reduced metal and mineral surfaces generate mixed C4 oxygenates.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"FTT-type CO/H2 chemistry on Fe-Ni surfaces is a chemically plausible but weakly supported alternative source of branched aldehydes. The cited literature reports amino-acid formation from CO/H2/NH3 over Fe-Ni catalysts but does not robustly establish isobutyraldehyde itself; this node is therefore explicitly speculative and included only as a contested upstream skeleton source (Yoshino et al., 1971; NASA TM X-62,411, 1975; Glavin et al., 2010; Elsila et al., 2021).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker synthesis of valine aminonitrile from isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"evaporitic pool, aerosol film, or cyanide reservoir setting; ferrocyanide or Prussian Blue can buffer cyanide availability\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia condenses with the aldehyde to an imine or iminium that is trapped by cyanide, giving the alpha-aminonitrile and releasing water overall.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The Strecker end-game from isobutyraldehyde is one of the strongest prebiotic routes to valine or its aminonitrile precursor. Valine has been made in Strecker-style systems using ferrocyanide-based cyanide storage, and this chemistry also underlies the aqueous processing of spark-discharge aldehydes and HCN (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013; Ring et al., 1972; Johnson et al., 2009). The major uncertainty lies upstream in making and preserving isobutyraldehyde.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of valine aminonitrile to valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"prolonged hydration, geothermal warming, or repeated wet-dry and rehydration cycles\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Stepwise nitrile hydration and hydrolysis convert the aminonitrile to the amino acid; ammonia is the net coproduct.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydrolysis is the standard completion step of Strecker chemistry and converts the experimentally accessible valine aminonitrile into free valine under sustained aqueous heating. It is the natural end of both classical Strecker routes and the Patel/Sutherland valine branch (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013; Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Ni-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"pH about 8; mineral-interface aqueous system; laboratory proof used as mild analog for H2-fed prebiotic mineral chemistry\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The ketoacid forms an imine with ammonia and the imine is hydrogenated on nickel, competing with but not eliminating ketoacid reduction.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. directly demonstrated alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine under H2 and ammonia on Ni catalysts, and reported no conversion without catalyst. This is one of the most secure valine-specific end-games, but it deliberately leaves the prebiotic origin of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid unresolved because the literature does not yet solve that problem (Kaur et al., 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Pd/C-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Pd/C\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Pd/C\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"pH about 8; high amino-acid selectivity relative to hydroxy-acid side products\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Pd-catalyzed imine hydrogenation efficiently channels the ketoacid toward the amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pd/C gave the highest amino-acid selectivity in the alpha-ketoacid reductive amination study of Kaur et al., including the alpha-KIV to valine conversion. Palladium is geochemically rarer than Ni, so this node is best viewed as an experimentally strong benchmark rather than the most abundant natural catalyst (Kaur et al., 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe-Ni meteorite-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"ground Fe-Ni meteorite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aletai meteorite powder\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"pH about 8; heterogeneous metal grains as catalytic interfaces\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Imine formation is followed by hydrogen transfer on meteoritic Fe-Ni metal surfaces.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. showed that ground Aletai iron meteorite catalyzes reductive amination of biological alpha-ketoacids, including alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine. This makes the end-game geochemically attractive because Fe-Ni metal is plausible in localized meteoritic or serpentinization-linked settings (Kaur et al., 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"FeS/H2S-mediated reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"freshly precipitated FeS\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"moderate\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"sulfide-rich vent wall or subseafloor pore; pH near the NH4+/NH3 transition; coupling to pyrite-forming chemistry\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia forms an imine with the ketoacid and Fe-S redox chemistry drives reduction while sulfide is oxidized to elemental sulfur overall.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"FeS/H2S systems were reported to promote reductive amination of alpha-oxo acids in metabolism-first experiments, with electron flow coupled to sulfide and pyrite chemistry. Alpha-ketoisovalerate was not the headline substrate in those papers, so this valine-specific node is weaker than the Ni, Pd, and meteorite routes but remains chemically grounded in the demonstrated Fe-S reaction class (Hafenbradl et al., 1995; Huber and Wachtershauser, 2003).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe(OH)2-mediated reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"fresh Fe(OH)2\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"moderate H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe(OH)2\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"alkaline Fe2+-rich mineral interface; high ammonia favored; net written against environmental H2\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide-rich Fe(II) surfaces assist imine formation and reduction to the amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Fresh Fe(OH)2 was reported to promote reductive amination of alpha-keto acids in the Fe(II)-based studies of Huber and Wachtershauser, and iron oxyhydroxide systems later showed analogous amino-acid formation from pyruvate. The original Fe(OH)2 experiments used Fe(II)-rich precipitates as the immediate electron source; the present balanced node is written against environmental H2 to keep the network comparable, so it is more speculative than the direct Ni, Pd, and meteorite demonstrations (Huber and Wachtershauser, 2003; Barge et al., 2019).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs formation of valine hydantoin from alpha-ketoisovaleric acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"bicarbonate-buffered prebiotic pool\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide and ammonia add to the ketoacid and cyclize under carbonate-buffered aqueous conditions to the hydantoin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pulletikurti et al. showed that alpha-ketoacids undergo Bucherer-Bergs chemistry efficiently in bicarbonate buffer, forming hydantoins that can later hydrolyze to amino acids. Applying that reaction class to alpha-ketoisovaleric acid is chemically well motivated, although the literature still leaves alpha-KIV supply unresolved and the broader cyanide-availability critique remains (Pulletikurti et al., 2022; Kaur et al., 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of valine hydantoin to valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"geothermal heating-cooling cycles or prolonged warm-pool hydrolysis\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ring opening and hydrolysis of the hydantoin release the amino acid together with ammonia and carbon dioxide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoin hydrolysis is the slower completion step of the Bucherer-Bergs route and was demonstrated at elevated temperature in the alpha-ketoacid study of Pulletikurti et al. This provides a conceptually distinct late-stage route to valine from the same alpha-keto hub (Pulletikurti et al., 2022).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone cyanosulfidic-Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetone is trapped as its cyanohydrin, converted by sulfide, copper, and UV chemistry to isobutyraldehyde, then carried through Strecker chemistry and hydrolysis to valine. This is the most experimentally elaborated surface branch to a valine precursor, though staging and sulfide supply are debated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin reservoir completion pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"If acetone cyanohydrin is already accumulated from upstream cyanide chemistry, only the sulfide-driven conversion and Strecker finishing steps are needed to reach valine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal CO-to-Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"A speculative Fe-Ni carbon monoxide hydrogenation branch supplies isobutyraldehyde, which after quenching and transfer into a cyanide-rich surface pool enters the experimentally strong Strecker end-game.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct Strecker end-game from isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"description\": \"Any prior source of isobutyraldehyde, including routes not expanded here, can feed the classical late-stage valine aminonitrile and hydrolysis sequence.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Nickel-catalyzed alpha-KIV reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Assuming alpha-ketoisovaleric acid is available, nickel under H2 and ammonia gives the best-studied direct catalytic conversion to valine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Pd/C-catalyzed alpha-KIV reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Assuming alpha-ketoisovaleric acid is available, Pd/C carries out the same end-game with especially high amino-acid selectivity.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Meteoritic Fe-Ni alpha-KIV reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Assuming alpha-ketoisovaleric acid is available, ground Fe-Ni meteorite substitutes for synthetic catalysts and still converts the ketoacid directly to valine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"FeS/H2S alpha-KIV reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"In a sulfur-rich vent-like setting, FeS and sulfide provide a metabolism-first style route from alpha-ketoisovaleric acid to valine; the reaction class is supported, but valine-specific evidence is less direct than for Ni or Pd.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe(OH)2 alpha-KIV reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Fresh Fe(OH)2 offers an Fe2+-rich mineral-interface alternative to Ni and Pd for the final amination step, again assuming alpha-ketoisovaleric acid is available.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Alpha-ketoisovaleric acid can also reach valine through cyanide and ammonia capture as valine hydantoin, followed by warm aqueous hydrolysis. This route is chemically distinct from direct reductive amination but inherits the same alpha-KIV bottleneck.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_015\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Valine", "overall_strategy": "Valine is treated as a skeleton-limited target. The network therefore centers the two experimentally strongest late-stage solutions: classical Strecker chemistry on isobutyraldehyde and direct conversion of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid by mineral/metal-mediated reductive amination or by Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin chemistry. Upstream branched-skeleton supply is kept explicit and evidence-weighted: a surface acetone-to-isobutyraldehyde cyanosulfidic sequence is included because Patel-type chemistry reaches the valine aminonitrile branch, while a CO/H2 Fe-Ni route is retained only as a flagged hydrothermal alternative. Alpha-ketoisovaleric acid itself is left as a supplied hub intermediate because the literature identifies its abiotic synthesis as unresolved. Known competing sinks to pantoate and alpha-hydroxyisovalerate are acknowledged in the literature but not expanded here so the network remains within the requested size cap.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CO", "iupac_name": "carbon monoxide", "common_name": "carbon monoxide", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "sulfane", "common_name": "hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C3H6O", "iupac_name": "propan-2-one", "common_name": "acetone", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C4H7NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanenitrile", "common_name": "acetone cyanohydrin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C4H8O", "iupac_name": "2-methylpropanal", "common_name": "isobutyraldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C5H10N2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile", "common_name": "valine aminonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C5H8O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxo-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "alpha-ketoisovaleric acid (alpha-KIV)", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C6H10N2O2", "iupac_name": "5-(propan-2-yl)imidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "valine hydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "S", "iupac_name": "sulfur", "common_name": "elemental sulfur", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C5H11NO2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "valine", "inchi": null, "role": "target", "environment_formed": null}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Acetone cyanohydrin formation", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "cyanide-rich evaporitic or geothermal pool; cyanohydrin capture favored during concentration and limited dilution"}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds nucleophilically to the ketone carbonyl and the alkoxide is protonated to give the tertiary cyanohydrin.", "reasoning": "This is the entry step of the Patel/Sutherland branched-valine branch and is straightforward cyanohydrin chemistry under cyanide-bearing surface conditions (Patel et al., 2015; Liu et al., 2023). It is retained because it is one of the few experimentally grounded ways to begin assembling the branched valine skeleton from simple carbonyl chemistry."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Cyanosulfidic conversion of acetone cyanohydrin to isobutyraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004", "mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox pair"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I)/Cu(II) salts", "aqueous": "true", "other": "short-wave UV irradiation; HS-/H2S as stoichiometric reductant; staged delivery in shallow pools or streams"}, "mechanism": "The cyanohydrin is sulfurized through thioamide-like intermediates and then photoredox-driven deoxygenation and rearrangement deliver the aldehyde oxidation state.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. showed the acetone branch can be carried through sulfur chemistry and Cu photoredox to the valine aminonitrile level. This node is a mass-balanced summary of that staged sequence, keeping the required sulfide and UV explicit. The branch is experimentally important but geochemically contested because it needs localized sulfide, UV, and reagent staging (Patel et al., 2015; Liu et al., 2023; Ranjan et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "FTT-like CO hydrogenation to isobutyraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_005"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Fe-Ni alloy", "magnetite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "450-550K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "Fe-Ni alloy/magnetite", "aqueous": "false", "other": "subseafloor gas-water-mineral interface; rapid quenching needed to preserve aldehydes"}, "mechanism": "Surface-mediated carbonyl coupling and hydrogenation on reduced metal and mineral surfaces generate mixed C4 oxygenates.", "reasoning": "FTT-type CO/H2 chemistry on Fe-Ni surfaces is a chemically plausible but weakly supported alternative source of branched aldehydes. The cited literature reports amino-acid formation from CO/H2/NH3 over Fe-Ni catalysts but does not robustly establish isobutyraldehyde itself; this node is therefore explicitly speculative and included only as a contested upstream skeleton source (Yoshino et al., 1971; NASA TM X-62,411, 1975; Glavin et al., 2010; Elsila et al., 2021)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Strecker synthesis of valine aminonitrile from isobutyraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_003", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "295-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "evaporitic pool, aerosol film, or cyanide reservoir setting; ferrocyanide or Prussian Blue can buffer cyanide availability"}, "mechanism": "Ammonia condenses with the aldehyde to an imine or iminium that is trapped by cyanide, giving the alpha-aminonitrile and releasing water overall.", "reasoning": "The Strecker end-game from isobutyraldehyde is one of the strongest prebiotic routes to valine or its aminonitrile precursor. Valine has been made in Strecker-style systems using ferrocyanide-based cyanide storage, and this chemistry also underlies the aqueous processing of spark-discharge aldehydes and HCN (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013; Ring et al., 1972; Johnson et al., 2009). The major uncertainty lies upstream in making and preserving isobutyraldehyde."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Hydrolysis of valine aminonitrile to valine", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "prolonged hydration, geothermal warming, or repeated wet-dry and rehydration cycles"}, "mechanism": "Stepwise nitrile hydration and hydrolysis convert the aminonitrile to the amino acid; ammonia is the net coproduct.", "reasoning": "Hydrolysis is the standard completion step of Strecker chemistry and converts the experimentally accessible valine aminonitrile into free valine under sustained aqueous heating. It is the natural end of both classical Strecker routes and the Patel/Sutherland valine branch (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013; Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Ni-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_005"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ni/SiO2-Al2O3"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295-320K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni/SiO2-Al2O3", "aqueous": "true", "other": "pH about 8; mineral-interface aqueous system; laboratory proof used as mild analog for H2-fed prebiotic mineral chemistry"}, "mechanism": "The ketoacid forms an imine with ammonia and the imine is hydrogenated on nickel, competing with but not eliminating ketoacid reduction.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. directly demonstrated alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine under H2 and ammonia on Ni catalysts, and reported no conversion without catalyst. This is one of the most secure valine-specific end-games, but it deliberately leaves the prebiotic origin of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid unresolved because the literature does not yet solve that problem (Kaur et al., 2024)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Pd/C-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_005"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Pd/C"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295-320K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Pd/C", "aqueous": "true", "other": "pH about 8; high amino-acid selectivity relative to hydroxy-acid side products"}, "mechanism": "Pd-catalyzed imine hydrogenation efficiently channels the ketoacid toward the amino acid.", "reasoning": "Pd/C gave the highest amino-acid selectivity in the alpha-ketoacid reductive amination study of Kaur et al., including the alpha-KIV to valine conversion. Palladium is geochemically rarer than Ni, so this node is best viewed as an experimentally strong benchmark rather than the most abundant natural catalyst (Kaur et al., 2024)."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Fe-Ni meteorite-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_005"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["ground Fe-Ni meteorite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295-320K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Aletai meteorite powder", "aqueous": "true", "other": "pH about 8; heterogeneous metal grains as catalytic interfaces"}, "mechanism": "Imine formation is followed by hydrogen transfer on meteoritic Fe-Ni metal surfaces.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. showed that ground Aletai iron meteorite catalyzes reductive amination of biological alpha-ketoacids, including alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine. This makes the end-game geochemically attractive because Fe-Ni metal is plausible in localized meteoritic or serpentinization-linked settings (Kaur et al., 2024)."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "FeS/H2S-mediated reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_014", "mol_005"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["freshly precipitated FeS"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370K", "pressure": "moderate", "catalyst": "FeS", "aqueous": "true", "other": "sulfide-rich vent wall or subseafloor pore; pH near the NH4+/NH3 transition; coupling to pyrite-forming chemistry"}, "mechanism": "Ammonia forms an imine with the ketoacid and Fe-S redox chemistry drives reduction while sulfide is oxidized to elemental sulfur overall.", "reasoning": "FeS/H2S systems were reported to promote reductive amination of alpha-oxo acids in metabolism-first experiments, with electron flow coupled to sulfide and pyrite chemistry. Alpha-ketoisovalerate was not the headline substrate in those papers, so this valine-specific node is weaker than the Ni, Pd, and meteorite routes but remains chemically grounded in the demonstrated Fe-S reaction class (Hafenbradl et al., 1995; Huber and Wachtershauser, 2003)."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Fe(OH)2-mediated reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_005"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["fresh Fe(OH)2"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-360K", "pressure": "moderate H2", "catalyst": "Fe(OH)2", "aqueous": "true", "other": "alkaline Fe2+-rich mineral interface; high ammonia favored; net written against environmental H2"}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide-rich Fe(II) surfaces assist imine formation and reduction to the amino acid.", "reasoning": "Fresh Fe(OH)2 was reported to promote reductive amination of alpha-keto acids in the Fe(II)-based studies of Huber and Wachtershauser, and iron oxyhydroxide systems later showed analogous amino-acid formation from pyruvate. The original Fe(OH)2 experiments used Fe(II)-rich precipitates as the immediate electron source; the present balanced node is written against environmental H2 to keep the network comparable, so it is more speculative than the direct Ni, Pd, and meteorite demonstrations (Huber and Wachtershauser, 2003; Barge et al., 2019)."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Bucherer-Bergs formation of valine hydantoin from alpha-ketoisovaleric acid", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_003", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_005"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "295-310K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "bicarbonate-buffered prebiotic pool"}, "mechanism": "Cyanide and ammonia add to the ketoacid and cyclize under carbonate-buffered aqueous conditions to the hydantoin.", "reasoning": "Pulletikurti et al. showed that alpha-ketoacids undergo Bucherer-Bergs chemistry efficiently in bicarbonate buffer, forming hydantoins that can later hydrolyze to amino acids. Applying that reaction class to alpha-ketoisovaleric acid is chemically well motivated, although the literature still leaves alpha-KIV supply unresolved and the broader cyanide-availability critique remains (Pulletikurti et al., 2022; Kaur et al., 2024)."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Hydrolysis of valine hydantoin to valine", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_004", "mol_013"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "350-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "geothermal heating-cooling cycles or prolonged warm-pool hydrolysis"}, "mechanism": "Ring opening and hydrolysis of the hydantoin release the amino acid together with ammonia and carbon dioxide.", "reasoning": "Hydantoin hydrolysis is the slower completion step of the Bucherer-Bergs route and was demonstrated at elevated temperature in the alpha-ketoacid study of Pulletikurti et al. This provides a conceptually distinct late-stage route to valine from the same alpha-keto hub (Pulletikurti et al., 2022)."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Acetone cyanosulfidic-Strecker pathway", "description": "Acetone is trapped as its cyanohydrin, converted by sulfide, copper, and UV chemistry to isobutyraldehyde, then carried through Strecker chemistry and hydrolysis to valine. This is the most experimentally elaborated surface branch to a valine precursor, though staging and sulfide supply are debated.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Cyanohydrin reservoir completion pathway", "description": "If acetone cyanohydrin is already accumulated from upstream cyanide chemistry, only the sulfide-driven conversion and Strecker finishing steps are needed to reach valine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Hydrothermal CO-to-Strecker pathway", "description": "A speculative Fe-Ni carbon monoxide hydrogenation branch supplies isobutyraldehyde, which after quenching and transfer into a cyanide-rich surface pool enters the experimentally strong Strecker end-game.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Direct Strecker end-game from isobutyraldehyde", "description": "Any prior source of isobutyraldehyde, including routes not expanded here, can feed the classical late-stage valine aminonitrile and hydrolysis sequence.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Nickel-catalyzed alpha-KIV reductive amination", "description": "Assuming alpha-ketoisovaleric acid is available, nickel under H2 and ammonia gives the best-studied direct catalytic conversion to valine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Pd/C-catalyzed alpha-KIV reductive amination", "description": "Assuming alpha-ketoisovaleric acid is available, Pd/C carries out the same end-game with especially high amino-acid selectivity.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Meteoritic Fe-Ni alpha-KIV reductive amination", "description": "Assuming alpha-ketoisovaleric acid is available, ground Fe-Ni meteorite substitutes for synthetic catalysts and still converts the ketoacid directly to valine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "FeS/H2S alpha-KIV reductive amination", "description": "In a sulfur-rich vent-like setting, FeS and sulfide provide a metabolism-first style route from alpha-ketoisovaleric acid to valine; the reaction class is supported, but valine-specific evidence is less direct than for Ni or Pd.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Fe(OH)2 alpha-KIV reductive amination", "description": "Fresh Fe(OH)2 offers an Fe2+-rich mineral-interface alternative to Ni and Pd for the final amination step, again assuming alpha-ketoisovaleric acid is available.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin pathway", "description": "Alpha-ketoisovaleric acid can also reach valine through cyanide and ammonia capture as valine hydantoin, followed by warm aqueous hydrolysis. This route is chemically distinct from direct reductive amination but inherits the same alpha-KIV bottleneck.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_004", "mol_009", "mol_011", "mol_015"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_004", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_002", "rxn_005", "rxn_012"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_011"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_009", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_004"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_015", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_012"], "outgoing_reactions": []}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 566548.2411384583, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T22:06:00.089795", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Valine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Valine is treated as a skeleton-limited target. The network therefore centers the two experimentally strongest late-stage solutions: classical Strecker chemistry on isobutyraldehyde and direct conversion of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid by mineral/metal-mediated reductive amination or by Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin chemistry. Upstream branched-skeleton supply is kept explicit and evidence-weighted: a surface acetone-to-isobutyraldehyde cyanosulfidic sequence is included because Patel-type chemistry reaches the valine aminonitrile branch, while a CO/H2 Fe-Ni route is retained only as a flagged hydrothermal alternative. Alpha-ketoisovaleric acid itself is left as a supplied hub intermediate because the literature identifies its abiotic synthesis as unresolved. Known competing sinks to pantoate and alpha-hydroxyisovalerate are acknowledged in the literature but not expanded here so the network remains within the requested size cap.\n\nMOLECULES (15 nodes):\n  - mol_001: CO (carbon monoxide) [starting]\n  - mol_002: H2 (hydrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_003: HCN (hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_004: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_005: H2O (water) [starting]\n  - mol_006: H2S (hydrogen sulfide) [starting]\n  - mol_007: C3H6O (acetone) [starting]\n  - mol_008: C4H7NO (acetone cyanohydrin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_009: C4H8O (isobutyraldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_010: C5H10N2 (valine aminonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_011: C5H8O3 (alpha-ketoisovaleric acid (alpha-KIV)) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C6H10N2O2 (valine hydantoin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_013: CO2 (carbon dioxide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: S (elemental sulfur) [intermediate]\n  - mol_015: C5H11NO2 (valine) [target]\n\nREACTIONS (12 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_007, mol_003 -> mol_008 (Acetone cyanohydrin formation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_008, mol_006 -> mol_009, mol_004, mol_014 (Cyanosulfidic conversion of acetone cyanohydrin to isobutyraldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_001, mol_002 -> mol_009, mol_005 (FTT-like CO hydrogenation to isobutyraldehyde) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_004: mol_009, mol_003, mol_004 -> mol_010, mol_005 (Strecker synthesis of valine aminonitrile from isobutyraldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_010, mol_005 -> mol_015, mol_004 (Hydrolysis of valine aminonitrile to valine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_006: mol_011, mol_004, mol_002 -> mol_015, mol_005 (Ni-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_007: mol_011, mol_004, mol_002 -> mol_015, mol_005 (Pd/C-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_008: mol_011, mol_004, mol_002 -> mol_015, mol_005 (Fe-Ni meteorite-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_009: mol_011, mol_004, mol_006 -> mol_015, mol_014, mol_005 (FeS/H2S-mediated reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_010: mol_011, mol_004, mol_002 -> mol_015, mol_005 (Fe(OH)2-mediated reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_011: mol_011, mol_003, mol_004 -> mol_012, mol_005 (Bucherer-Bergs formation of valine hydantoin from alpha-ketoisovaleric acid) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_012: mol_012, mol_005 -> mol_015, mol_004, mol_013 (Hydrolysis of valine hydantoin to valine) [Biochemical]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Acetone cyanosulfidic-Strecker pathway (4 steps)\n  - P2: Cyanohydrin reservoir completion pathway (3 steps)\n  - P3: Hydrothermal CO-to-Strecker pathway (3 steps)\n  - P4: Direct Strecker end-game from isobutyraldehyde (2 steps)\n  - P5: Nickel-catalyzed alpha-KIV reductive amination (1 steps)\n  - P6: Pd/C-catalyzed alpha-KIV reductive amination (1 steps)\n  - P7: Meteoritic Fe-Ni alpha-KIV reductive amination (1 steps)\n  - P8: FeS/H2S alpha-KIV reductive amination (1 steps)\n  - P9: Fe(OH)2 alpha-KIV reductive amination (1 steps)\n  - P10: Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin pathway (2 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 12 reactions | 8 balanced as written | 4 balanceable with coefficients | 0 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_002 balanceable: C4H7NO + 2 H2S -> C4H8O + NH3 + 2 S\n  - rxn_003 balanceable: 4 CO + 7 H2 -> C4H8O + 3 H2O\n  - rxn_005 balanceable: C5H10N2 + 2 H2O -> C5H11NO2 + NH3\n  - rxn_012 balanceable: C6H10N2O2 + 2 H2O -> C5H11NO2 + NH3 + CO2\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so the network can be corrected. Note that implausibly large inferred coefficients may also indicate a mis-specified reaction.\n=== END BALANCE CHECK ===\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH ON TARGET MOLECULE ===\nThe following research was conducted on the target molecule before network generation. Use it to assess whether the network aligns with known prebiotic chemistry literature.\n\n=== DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of Valine: A Cross-Framework Evidence Review\n\nValine is one of the most robustly \"prebiotically accessible\" proteinogenic amino acids at the level of detection \u2014 it appears in spark-discharge products, carbonaceous chondrites, asteroid-return samples and interstellar-ice photolysis residues \u2014 but no single route yet delivers it selectively, at high yield, from simple feedstocks under one set of geochemically defended conditions. The best-characterized end-game steps are (i) Strecker chemistry on isobutyraldehyde and (ii) metal-catalyzed reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate (\u03b1-KIV); the critical bottleneck in every framework is the prebiotic origin of the branched C4/C5 skeleton (isobutyraldehyde or \u03b1-KIV), and the biosynthetic pyruvate \u2192 acetolactate \u2192 \u03b1-KIV sequence has no published nonenzymatic demonstration.\n\n## TL;DR\n\n- **Valine forms easily but non-selectively.** It has been confirmed in spark-discharge products (Ring et al. 1972, PNAS; Johnson et al. 2009, OLEB reanalysis of Miller's original samples), in Murchison and other CM chondrites, in Bennu samples (Glavin et al. 2025, Nature Astronomy), and in UV-irradiated interstellar ice analogues (Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. 2002, Nature) \u2014 always as a minor, racemic component accompanied by its isomers norvaline and isovaline.\n- **Two end-game chemistries are experimentally solid; the skeleton is not.** Isobutyraldehyde + HCN + NH3 (Strecker) and \u03b1-KIV + NH3 + H2 over Ni, Pd or ground iron meteorite (Kaur et al. 2024, Chem; 22 \u00b0C, pH 8, no reaction without catalyst) both give valine, but how isobutyraldehyde or \u03b1-KIV arise prebiotically is either demonstrated only in a multistep, operator-staged network (Patel et al. 2015, Nature Chemistry: acetone \u2192 valine aminonitrile via HCN/H2S/UV/Cu) or not demonstrated at all (nonenzymatic acetolactate pathway).\n- **All routes are contested.** Spark discharge depends on reducing atmospheres; cyanosulfidic chemistry on high sulfide, 254-nm UV and staged reagent delivery; metabolism-first routes on a supply of \u03b1-KIV that nobody has made abiotically; and extraterrestrial delivery supplies only nmol/g-level valine. For network design, treat valine as a downstream \"skeleton-limited\" target and prioritize finding a prebiotic source of the C5 branched skeleton.\n\n## Key Findings\n\n| Route / framework | Feedstocks | Key valine-relevant step | Valine actually detected/produced? | Catalyst | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Spark discharge (Miller-Urey and variants) | CH4, N2/NH3, H2O, H2 (also CO/CO2 + H2) | Radical gas-phase chemistry \u2192 aldehydes + HCN \u2192 Strecker in aqueous phase | Yes (Ring et al. 1972; Johnson et al. 2009 reanalysis) | None required | Contested (atmospheric redox state) |\n| Strecker synthesis on isobutyraldehyde | Isobutyraldehyde, HCN, NH3 | \u03b1-aminonitrile \u2192 hydrolysis | Yes (textbook; prebiotic variants incl. ferrocyanide/aerosol, Ruiz-Bermejo et al. 2013) | None required | End-game solid; aldehyde origin contested |\n| Cyanosulfidic network (Sutherland) | HCN, H2S/HS\u207b, phosphate, Cu, 254-nm UV | Acetone \u2192 cyanohydrin \u2192 reductive homologation \u2192 valine \u03b1-aminonitrile | Valine precursor (aminonitrile) shown; no yield stated | Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox, HS\u207b reductant | Contested (sulfide levels, UV, staging) |\n| Reductive amination of \u03b1-KIV | \u03b1-KIV, NH3/NH4\u207a, H2 (or formate) | Imine formation + hydrogenation | Yes (Kaur et al. 2024, Chem) | Ni/SiO2-Al2O3, Pd/C, Aletai iron meteorite; none = no reaction | End-game solid; \u03b1-KIV origin unsolved |\n| Bucherer\u2013Bergs on \u03b1-ketoacids | \u03b1-ketoacid, HCN, NH3, CO2 | Hydantoin \u2192 hydrolysis | General for \u03b1-ketoacids (Pulletikurti et al. 2022, Nature Chemistry) | None (bicarbonate buffer) | Requires \u03b1-KIV; cyanide relevance debated |\n| Biosynthetic analog (pyruvate \u2192 acetolactate \u2192 DHIV \u2192 \u03b1-KIV) | Pyruvate | ThDP condensation, reductoisomerization, dehydration | **No nonenzymatic demonstration** | \u2014 | Hypothetical |\n| Fischer\u2013Tropsch-type (FTT) | CO, H2, NH3 | Surface catalysis at 200\u2013500 \u00b0C | \"Several \u03b1-amino acids\" (Yoshino et al. 1971); not reproduced robustly | Fe\u2013Ni metal, magnetite | Contested |\n| HCN polymer hydrolysis | HCN | Oligomer hydrolysis | Not in standard HCN oligomers (Ferris et al. 1974); reported after \u2076\u2070Co \u03b3-irradiation of HCN | None | Weak for valine |\n| Interstellar ice UV photolysis | H2O, CH3OH, NH3, CO, CO2 (\u00b1HCN) | Radical chemistry at 12 K, hydrolysis of residue | Yes in Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. 2002; ee measured by Modica et al. 2014 | None (UV) | Accepted as possible source of meteoritic organics |\n| Impact shock | Comet-ice analogue | Shock heating | Not reported (glycine, alanine, AIB, isovaline only; Martins et al. 2013) | None | Not shown for valine |\n| Meteoritic/asteroidal delivery | Parent-body aqueous chemistry | Strecker/cyanohydrin in parent body | Yes, nmol/g level; racemic or contested L-excess | Parent-body minerals | Occurrence accepted; contribution debated |\n\n## Details\n\n### 1. Spark-discharge / Miller-Urey-type experiments\n\n**What was shown.** Ring, Wolman, Friedmann and Miller (1972, *PNAS* 69:765) ran electric discharges on CH4 + N2 + H2O with traces of NH3 and confirmed glycine, alanine, \u03b1-amino-n-butyric acid, \u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid, **valine, norvaline, isovaline**, leucine, isoleucine, alloisoleucine, norleucine, proline, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, serine, threonine and others.\\[1\\]\\[2\\] This is the classic demonstration that the hydrophobic branched amino acids form alongside their unbranched isomers \u2014 valine is produced together with norvaline and isovaline, so any analysis must resolve the C5 isomers chromatographically.\n\n**Archived-sample reanalyses.** After Stanley Miller's death in 2007, Bada and colleagues found his dried 1950s residues. Johnson et al. (2008, *Science* 322:404) showed that the \"volcanic\" apparatus (an aspirator injecting steam into the spark flask) gave 22 amino acids and five amines, more diverse than the classic apparatus. Johnson et al. (2009, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres* 39:240) reported that the original samples contain several aminobutyric acid isomers plus serine, homoserine, isoserine, isovaline, **valine**, phenylalanine, ornithine and others, and that both diversity and yield increased with the water-aspirating device. Parker et al. (2011, *PNAS*) analyzed an unpublished 1958 H2S-rich experiment (CH4, NH3, H2S, CO2), reporting the first sulfur amino acids from spark experiments and the highest relative yields of aminobutyric acid isomers ever found in a spark experiment; that paper's emphasis is on sulfur and C4 amino acids, and this review does not rely on it for a valine-specific yield.\\[3\\]\n\n**Conditions and yields.** Ambient temperature, 1 bar-scale gas mixtures, electrical energy; no catalyst. Schlesinger and Miller (1983, *Journal of Molecular Evolution*) showed that total amino acid yields are 1.2\u20134.7% (based on carbon) for CH4-containing mixtures largely independent of H2/CH4 ratio, about 2% for H2 + CO or H2 + CO2 mixtures at H2/C ratios of 2\u20134, but only ~10\u207b\u00b3% when H2/CO2 = 0.\\[4\\] Higher amino acids such as valine are a small fraction of the total.\n\n**Mechanism.** Discharge produces HCN, aldehydes and ketones in the gas phase; amino acids form in the aqueous trap by Strecker (aldehyde + HCN + NH3) and related cyanohydrin chemistry. Valine thus derives from isobutyraldehyde formed by gas-phase radical recombination.\n\n**Critiques.** The main objection is atmospheric redox state: if the Hadean atmosphere was CO2/N2-dominated, yields collapse (the 10\u207b\u00b3% figure above). Defenders argue that volcanic plumes (Johnson et al. 2008) or transiently H2-rich post-impact atmospheres provide local reducing conditions. A second critique is selectivity: valine is a minor product in a broad mixture with non-proteinogenic isomers.\n\n### 2. Strecker synthesis from isobutyraldehyde \u2014 and the origin of isobutyraldehyde\n\n**Chemistry.** Isobutyraldehyde (2-methylpropanal) + NH3 + HCN \u2192 2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile (valine \u03b1-aminonitrile) \u2192 hydrolysis \u2192 racemic valine. No catalyst is required; the reaction proceeds in water at ambient temperature, with the aminonitrile/cyanohydrin partition controlled by NH3/HCN ratios and pH. Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*) used aqueous aerosols with UV and ferrocyanide or Prussian Blue as the cyanide source to perform Strecker syntheses of alanine, valine and phenylalanine, presenting ferrocyanides as a way to store cyanide in an Fe(II)-bearing ocean.\n\n**Competing sinks for isobutyraldehyde.** Isobutyraldehyde is also the precursor of pantoic acid: Miller and Schlesinger (1993, *Journal of Molecular Evolution*) reported that pantoic acid forms in good prebiotic yield from isobutyraldehyde (or \u03b1-KIV) + H2CO + HCN. Fairchild et al. (2024, *Science*; Powner group) showed a phosphate-catalyzed, neutral-pH aldol of formaldehyde with isobutyraldehyde \u2014 the Strecker precursors of glycine and valine \u2014 selectively leading to pantolactone/pantetheine. This shows that, in a mixture rich in formaldehyde, isobutyraldehyde is partly diverted from valine toward coenzyme A precursors; network designers should model this branch explicitly.\n\n**Critiques.** Strecker chemistry itself is uncontroversial; the open question is the supply of isobutyraldehyde. Proposed sources include spark/plume chemistry (above), the cyanosulfidic homologation of acetone (below), FTT chemistry, and decarboxylation of \u03b1-KIV (which presupposes \u03b1-KIV). Dilute HCN is a further problem: Stribling and Miller (1987, *Origins of Life and Evolution of the Biosphere* 17:261) calculated, for an HCN production rate of 100 nmol cm\u207b\u00b2 yr\u207b\u00b9, a steady-state primitive-ocean HCN concentration of about 4 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2076 M at pH 8 and 0 \u00b0C, pushing Strecker chemistry toward evaporitic, eutectic-freezing or ferrocyanide-storage scenarios.\n\n### 3. Cyanosulfidic / systems-chemistry route (Sutherland group)\n\n**What was shown.** Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy and Sutherland (2015, *Nature Chemistry* 7:301) derived precursors of ribonucleotides, amino acids and lipids by reductive homologation of HCN and its derivatives. Valine is accessible: dihydroxyacetone reduction gives acetone; acetone + HCN gives a cyanohydrin; reaction with H2S gives an \u03b1-hydroxythioamide; Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox cycling under 254-nm UV with HS\u207b as stoichiometric reductant removes the hydroxyl to give the isobutyraldehyde-level product, which is trapped as a cyanohydrin and converted by NH3 to the **valine \u03b1-aminonitrile**. The authors demonstrated the conversion of the constitutionally related intermediate to the valine aminonitrile by addition of ammonia and noted that a further homologation cycle gives the leucine aminonitrile. The network accesses 12 amino acids via Strecker intermediates. A later paper (Liu et al. 2023, *Nature Chemistry*, \"Thiophosphate photochemistry enables prebiotic access to sugars and terpenoid precursors\") showed thiophosphate (PSO3\u00b3\u207b) photochemistry improves parts of this network and preserves access to valine and leucine.\n\n**Yields.** Patel et al. explicitly decline to give discrete yields for individual products, arguing that in a multi-product network the yield of any one compound is less meaningful; no percentage is published for the valine aminonitrile from acetone. Benchmark homologation steps in related Sutherland-group work (e.g., glycolaldehyde ~42% after 4 h with CuCN/H2S under UV) indicate individual steps can be efficient, but the cumulative yield of the multistep valine sequence is unknown.\n\n**Environment proposed.** Surface streams and pools on an early Earth with UV exposure, cyanide stored as ferrocyanide salts (released by impact/thermal processing), sulfide from volcanism or impacts, and \"confluence\" of streams delivering different intermediates.\n\n**Critiques.** (i) Sulfide availability: Ranjan, Todd, Sutherland and Sasselov (2018, *Astrobiology* 18:1023) concluded that volcanic outgassing \"could have supplied prebiotically relevant levels of SO2-derived anions, but not H2S-derived anions,\" and that \"epochs of moderately high volcanism could have been especially conducive to cyanosulfidic prebiotic chemistry,\" so the chemistry needs specific local settings (e.g., impact-supplied sulfide or high-outgassing epochs). (ii) The use of 254-nm UV and Cu catalysts, and stepwise reagent addition by the experimenter, is criticized as idealized relative to a natural setting. (iii) Congruence with biochemistry: the route reaches valine via aminonitriles, not via \u03b1-KIV and transamination as in extant metabolism. (iv) Comment literature notes the chemistry requires H2S from the outset, possibly corresponding to a later geochemical stage.\n\n### 4. Reductive amination and transamination of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate (metal/mineral catalysis)\n\n**Strongest valine-specific demonstration.** Kaur, Rauscher, Werner, Song, Yi, Kaz\u00f6ne, Martin, T\u00fcys\u00fcz and Moran (2024, *Chem* 10:1) showed that H2 drives nonenzymatic reductive amination of six biological \u03b1-ketoacids \u2014 pyruvate, \u03b1-ketoglutarate, oxaloacetate, \u03b1-ketoisocaproate, **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate**, \u03b1-keto-\u03b2-methylvalerate \u2014 and glyoxylate to glycine, alanine, glutamate, aspartate, leucine, **valine** and isoleucine.\\[5\\] Conditions: 30 mM ketoacid, 5 bar H2, NH4HCO3 (5 equiv, 150 mM), pH 8 water, 22 \u00b0C, 72 h, with Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 (10 mol% Ni), Pd/C (1 mol%) or 3 mg ground Aletai iron meteorite (18 mol% Ni). With Ni and meteorite, combined amino + hydroxy acid yields were 6\u201384% at roughly 1:1 amino:hydroxy acid; with Pd, 52\u201388% with >5:1 selectivity for the amino acid in almost all cases. No reduction occurred without a metal catalyst. For pyruvate, ammonia as low as 6 mM gave alanine in up to 17% (Ni) or 44% (Pd) yield relative to ammonia \u2014 62 to 250 times lower ammonia than earlier prebiotic reductive aminations (375 mM\u20131.5 M). The same conditions run H2-driven reductive aldol chemistry that builds \u03b1-ketoglutarate-family ketoacids, but not the branched \u03b1-KIV skeleton. The authors cite 1\u201315 mmol/kg dissolved H2 in hydrothermal fluids as a geological analog to their H2 pressures.\n\n**Earlier iron-sulfide and iron-hydroxide work.** Hafenbradl, Keller, W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user and Stetter (1995, *Tetrahedron Letters*) coupled reductive amination of \u03b1-oxo acids to oxidative pyrite formation (FeS/H2S).\\[6\\]\\[7\\] Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003, *Tetrahedron Letters* 44:1695) reported efficient reductive amination of \u03b1-keto acids with freshly precipitated FeS or Fe(OH)2 at pH near the amine pKa, using stoichiometric Fe(II) reductant and high ammonia.\\[8\\] Barge, Flores, Baum, VanderVelde and Russell (2019, *PNAS* 116:4828) showed mixed-valence iron oxyhydroxides (\"green rust\") convert pyruvate to alanine and lactate; alanine is favored at intermediate Fe redox state (~50% Fe(II)), at pH above the NH3 pKa (~9.25), and at 25\u201370 \u00b0C, with no alanine at pH 7.\\[9\\]\\[10\\]\\[11\\] Kitadai et al. (2019, *Science Advances*) electroreduced metal sulfides (e.g., FeS \u2192 Fe\u2070) at potentials expected at alkaline vent\u2013seawater interfaces and achieved >50% yields for reductive amination of four keto acids (1.25 mM each) in one pot. These studies did not focus on \u03b1-KIV; valine is expected by analogy but was not the reported target in the iron-mineral papers cited here.\n\n**Reactivity data.** Mayer and Moran (2022, *Angewandte Chemie*) quantified nonenzymatic reduction and reductive amination of keto acids with BH3CN\u207b as a model hydride, showing \u03b1-ketoglutarate is the least reactive Krebs ketoacid toward amination\\[12\\] \u2014 relevant for predicting partitioning between \u03b1-KIV and other ketoacids in mixtures.\n\n**Critiques.** (i) Substrate supply: every reductive-amination demonstration starts from purchased \u03b1-KIV; no abiotic \u03b1-KIV synthesis exists (see \u00a75). (ii) Competing carbonyl reduction yields \u03b1-hydroxyisovalerate in roughly equal amounts with Ni/meteorite catalysts. (iii) Earlier Fe(II)-mediated work required 375 mM\u20131.5 M NH3, considered implausibly high; Kaur et al. reduce this but require concentrated metal catalysts (or Pd, which is rare). (iv) Metal-catalyzed hydrogenation is a textbook industrial reaction, and critics question whether native Ni or platinum-group metals were locally concentrated; proponents point to awaruite (Ni3Fe) in serpentinites, metallic Ni particles in H2-rich vents and ~220 tonnes/year of Fe-Ni cosmic dust.\n\n### 5. Metabolism-first: the biosynthetic analogy (pyruvate \u2192 acetolactate \u2192 \u03b1-KIV \u2192 valine)\n\nIn biology, acetohydroxyacid synthase (ThDP-dependent) condenses two pyruvates to (S)-2-acetolactate + CO2; ketol-acid reductoisomerase (Mg\u00b2\u207a/NADPH) performs an alkyl migration plus reduction to 2,3-dihydroxyisovalerate; dihydroxyacid dehydratase ([Fe\u2013S] enzyme) gives \u03b1-KIV; and transamination yields valine.\\[13\\]\\[14\\]\n\n**Evidence status.** A targeted literature search found **no peer-reviewed nonenzymatic demonstration** of any of the three skeleton-building steps. Thiazolium salts catalyze benzoin/acyloin condensations of aldehydes (Sheehan and Hara 1974, *Journal of Organic Chemistry*), but no prebiotic, metal- or mineral-catalyzed pyruvate \u2192 acetolactate conversion has been reported. Acetolactate is known to undergo nonenzymatic oxidative decarboxylation to diacetyl/acetoin, i.e., abiotic chemistry tends to degrade rather than build this intermediate. The reductoisomerization couples methyl migration and reduction in a single active site, with intermediates not released, which makes a nonenzymatic analog mechanistically demanding.\n\n**Iron-promoted networks.** Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019, *Nature* 569:104) showed Fe\u00b2\u207a in 70 \u00b0C water drives a pyruvate + glyoxylate network that reproduces 9 of 11 Krebs-cycle intermediates and all five universal metabolic precursors, and with hydroxylamine and metallic iron gives four amino acids (glycine, alanine, aspartate, glutamate); valine is not among them. Stubbs et al. (2020, *Nature Chemistry*) and Krishnamurthy-group cyanide-reductant chemistry (2022, *Nature Chemistry*) build \u03b1-ketoacid networks from glyoxylate and pyruvate, also without the branched-chain pathway.\n\n**Alternative evolutionary hypothesis.** Keefe, Lazcano and Miller (1995, *Origins of Life and Evolution of the Biosphere*) argued the acetolactate pathway cannot be the primordial route because it has unstable intermediates, and proposed that branched-chain amino acids were first made by reductive carboxylation of short branched-chain fatty acids (isobutyric acid \u2192 \u03b1-KIV) followed by transamination. This is a proposal, not an experimental demonstration.\n\n**Thermodynamics.** Amend and Shock (1998, *Science* 281:1659) computed the net synthesis 5CO2(aq) + NH4\u207a + 12H2(aq) \u2192 valine(aq) + H\u207a + 8H2O. They report that \"\u0394Gr is negative for 11 of the 20 net amino acid synthesis reactions in a 100\u00b0C mixed hydrothermal solution,\" whereas \"all 20 amino acids are strongly endergonic in cooler (18\u00b0C), oxidized, surface seawater\"; the paper's valine table row (entries of \u221270.12 and 178.00) was not verified here as to which quantity each represents, so per-valine kJ/mol values should be taken from the primary table. This establishes thermodynamic drive in H2-rich settings but says nothing about kinetic pathways; Aubrey, Cleaves and Bada (2009, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*) cautioned that such equilibrium models neglect kinetics and degradation, observing A \u21cc B \u21cc C (precursor \u21cc amino acid \u21cc degradation product) behavior at 125\u2013175 \u00b0C.\n\n**Critiques.** The framework predicts congruence with extant biochemistry (\u03b1-KIV \u2192 valine by amination), which the Kaur et al. data support for the final step, but the absence of a nonenzymatic \u03b1-KIV synthesis is the single largest gap in the metabolism-first account of valine. High temperatures also accelerate amino acid decomposition.\n\n### 6. Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry on \u03b1-ketoacids\n\nPulletikurti, Yadav, Springsteen and Krishnamurthy (2022, *Nature Chemistry* 14:1142) showed \u03b1-ketoacids react with cyanide and ammonia in bicarbonate buffer at room temperature via the Bucherer\u2013Bergs pathway to hydantoins, which hydrolyze (80 \u00b0C) to \u03b1-amino acids; e.g., \u03b1-ketoglutarate gave the hydantoin in 96% yield, with slower hydrolysis to glutamate (19% with 72% hydantoin remaining). The route bridges cyanide chemistry and ketoacid metabolism. For valine it would require \u03b1-KIV and so inherits the same skeleton problem. Kaur et al. (2024) criticize cyanide-based routes as incongruent with metabolism, since cyanide is never directly used in biochemistry and is toxic to metalloenzymes.\n\n### 7. Fischer\u2013Tropsch-type (FTT) and CO/H2 surface chemistry\n\nYoshino, Hayatsu and Anders (1971, *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta* 35:927) reported several \u03b1-amino acids and \u03b2-alanine from CO + H2 + NH3 over Fe\u2013Ni catalysts at 200\u2013500 \u00b0C, proposed as a solar-nebula source of meteoritic organics. A NASA follow-up (NASA TM X-62,411, 1975) heating CO/H2/NH3 with iron meteorite found only small quantities of a few amino acids, \"at odds with the existing literature.\" Glavin et al. (2010, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*) and Elsila et al. (2021, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*) note that FTT synthesis needs re-examination with modern analytics. Valine specifically has not been robustly shown by FTT; the route is contested.\n\n### 8. HCN polymer hydrolysis, formamide and radiolysis\n\nFerris, Wos, Nooner and Or\u00f3 (1974, *Journal of Molecular Evolution* 3:225) identified glycine, aspartic acid and diaminosuccinic acid as major and alanine, isoleucine and \u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid as minor amino acids from hydrolyzed HCN oligomers \u2014 valine is not among them. Ferris et al. (1978, *Journal of Molecular Evolution* 11:293) showed 0.1 M HCN oligomerizes at pH 9.2. Sweeney, Toste and Ponnamperuma (1976, *Origins of Life* 7:187) exposed aqueous HCN solutions (0.004\u20130.1 M) to cobalt-60 gamma rays and identified glycine, alanine, valine, serine, threonine, aspartic acid and glutamic acid among the hydrolysis products; Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013, *Life*) list 12 of 20 protein amino acids across HCN-polymer studies. Valine is therefore a minor, condition-dependent product of HCN polymer chemistry rather than a characteristic one. Formamide-based chemistry has been developed mainly for nucleobases; this review found no valine-specific formamide synthesis.\n\n### 9. Interstellar ice irradiation\n\nMu\u00f1oz Caro et al. (2002, *Nature* 416:403) UV-irradiated H2O:CH3OH:NH3:CO:CO2 (2:1:1:1:1) ice at 12 K and identified 16 amino acids in the hydrolyzed room-temperature residue, valine among them. Bernstein et al. (2002, *Nature* 416:401) independently found glycine, alanine and serine from H2O/CH3OH/NH3/HCN ices. Modica et al. (2014, *Astrophysical Journal*) measured small enantiomeric excesses induced by circularly polarized UV for five amino acids including valine and norvaline. Critique: products are detected only after acid hydrolysis, so precursors rather than free valine form in the ice; yields are tiny; relevance depends on delivery and survival.\n\n### 10. Impact/shock synthesis\n\nMartins, Price, Goldman, Sephton and Burchell (2013, *Nature Geoscience* 6:1045) fired projectiles at ~7 km/s into comet-ice analogues and obtained racemic alanine, \u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid and isovaline (plus precursors) after hydrolysis. Valine was not reported. Impact routes are therefore unproven for valine.\n\n### 11. Meteoritic occurrence and extraterrestrial delivery\n\n**Abundance.** Valine is a minor meteoritic amino acid. Glavin et al. (2021, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*) reported amino acids in Aguas Zarcas and Murchison at ~0.1 to 158 nmol/g overall; valine was at the low end, too low for compound-specific \u03b4\u00b9\u00b3C measurement. Bennu samples (Glavin et al. 2025, *Nature Astronomy* 9:199) contain 33 amino acids including 14 of the 20 protein amino acids, valine among them, with chiral non-protein amino acids racemic or nearly so; the same paper measured an L-valine excess of ~34% in an acid-hydrolyzed hot-water extract but also observed elevated L-valine in the procedural blank, so laboratory contamination is a possible explanation.\n\n**Enantiomeric data \u2014 contested.** Murchison's D/L valine ratio of 0.67 \u00b1 0.03 (\u224820% L-excess) in Glavin et al. (2021) differs from Aguas Zarcas and Murchison soil and may be extraterrestrial, but could not be confirmed isotopically. Pizzarello and Cronin (2000, *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta*) found \u03b1-H amino acids including valine racemic or attributed excesses to contamination, with the documented meteoritic excesses confined to \u03b1-methyl amino acids such as isovaline (up to 18.5% L in Murchison; Glavin and Dworkin 2009, *PNAS*). Dworkin et al. (2026, arXiv:2609.12249), an OSIRIS-REx contamination-knowledge paper, report that \"the hummocky sample shows an excess of ~20% L-valine (Kim et al. 2026),\" conclude that this excess \"is not attributable to spacecraft contamination,\" and cite only a modest 2.2% L-valine excess as the sole earlier chondrite report (Pizzarello and Cronin 2000). These literatures conflict; any valine L-excess in meteorites should be regarded as unconfirmed. In the primitive CM Asuka 12236, valine D/L ratios of ~0.82\u20130.89 indicate near-racemic composition (Glavin et al. 2020, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*).\n\n**Isomer context.** Meteorites contain valine together with norvaline, isovaline and other C5 amino acids, consistent with Strecker/cyanohydrin chemistry on parent bodies acting on mixtures of C4 aldehydes and ketones (isobutyraldehyde \u2192 valine; butanal \u2192 norvaline; 2-butanone \u2192 isovaline). The dominance of \u03b1-methyl amino acids such as isovaline in some chondrites contrasts with biology's selection of valine.\n\n**Critiques.** Delivery fluxes and survival through atmospheric entry/impact limit concentrations; nmol/g valine in meteorites cannot by itself sustain concentration-dependent chemistry without strong local accumulation.\n\n## Environmental Context\n\n- **Reducing atmospheres/volcanic plumes with lightning**: supported for valine by spark experiments, contingent on local reducing gas compositions (Johnson et al. 2008).\n- **UV-lit surface pools/streams with HCN, sulfide, Cu and phosphate (cyanosulfidic)**: valine aminonitrile demonstrated; sulfide and UV requirements are the main plausibility constraint, with Ranjan et al. (2018, *Astrobiology*) finding outgassing could supply SO2-derived but not H2S-derived anions at relevant levels.\n- **H2-rich hydrothermal/serpentinizing systems with Ni/Fe-Ni alloys or iron oxyhydroxides**: best supported for the final \u03b1-KIV \u2192 valine step (Kaur et al. 2024; Barge et al. 2019 for analogous pyruvate chemistry) and thermodynamically favorable (Amend and Shock 1998), but with no demonstrated \u03b1-KIV source.\n- **Meteorite parent bodies and interstellar ices**: proven valine sources at trace levels.\n\nOn the evidence, no single environment is demonstrated to make valine from simple feedstocks selectively. The most experimentally complete feedstock-to-valine chain is the cyanosulfidic acetone route (multistep, staged), while the most mild, catalytic and biochemically congruent final step is metal-catalyzed reductive amination.\n\n## Open Questions and Challenges\n\n1. **The branched-skeleton problem.** No abiotic synthesis of \u03b1-KIV is published; isobutyraldehyde arises only from non-selective discharge/FTT chemistry or the staged cyanosulfidic homologation of acetone. This is the central bottleneck for valine.\n2. **Nonenzymatic acetolactate pathway.** Pyruvate \u2192 acetolactate, reductoisomerization and dehydration all lack nonenzymatic demonstrations; finding thiazolium-free, metal-promoted pyruvate homocoupling would be a high-value experiment.\n3. **Selectivity versus isomers.** Valine co-forms with norvaline and isovaline in all non-enzymatic chemistries; mechanisms that favor the branched \u03b1-H isomer are unknown.\n4. **Concentration and compatibility.** HCN dilution (~4 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2076 M at pH 8 and 0 \u00b0C in Stribling and Miller's 1987 primitive-ocean estimate), the ammonia requirement for amination, sulfide levels for photoredox chemistry, and isobutyraldehyde diversion to pantoate/pantetheine are all unresolved compatibility issues.\n5. **Congruence with biochemistry.** Strecker and cyanosulfidic routes do not pass through \u03b1-KIV, while metabolism-first routes match biology's final step but not its skeleton-building steps.\n6. **Chirality.** Abiotic valine is racemic; claimed meteoritic and Bennu L-excesses are contradictory and unconfirmed.\n\n## Recommendations for Synthesis-Network Design\n\n- Model two independent entry points into valine: (a) isobutyraldehyde + HCN + NH3 (catalyst-free Strecker), and (b) \u03b1-KIV + NH3 + H2 over Ni/Fe-Ni (or formate over Pd). Treat both as demonstrated end-games.\n- Treat the upstream skeleton step as the explicit unknown: include the cyanosulfidic acetone \u2192 cyanohydrin \u2192 reductive homologation branch (with no published yield) and flag the biosynthetic acetolactate branch as hypothetical.\n- Include the formaldehyde + isobutyraldehyde \u2192 pantolactone side reaction and \u03b1-hydroxyisovalerate formation as competing sinks.\n- Track norvaline and isovaline as co-products and use chromatographic separation of C5 isomers in any experimental validation.\n- Prioritize experiments on nonenzymatic pyruvate homocoupling or reductive carboxylation of isobutyrate (Keefe et al. 1995 hypothesis) over further optimization of the final amination step.\n\n## Caveats\n\n- Several valine-specific numbers (e.g., per-compound yields in Kaur et al. 2024 and absolute valine yields in spark experiments) are reported here only as ranges or qualitative detections because individual values were not extracted from primary tables.\n- The Amend and Shock per-valine free energies were not verified against the primary table and should be checked before quantitative use; the \u2076\u2070Co-HCN valine detection is from Sweeney, Toste and Ponnamperuma (1976, *Origins of Life*).\n- Absence of a nonenzymatic acetolactate-pathway demonstration is based on targeted searches; an obscure report could exist.\n\n## Sources\n\n1. [Prebiotic Synthesis of Hydrophobic and Protein Amino Acids](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.69.3.765)\n2. [ChemInform Abstract: New Insights into Prebiotic Chemistry from Stanley Miller\u2032s Spark Discharge Experiments](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/235337989_ChemInform_Abstract_New_Insights_into_Prebiotic_Chemistry_from_Stanley_Miller's_Spark_Discharge_Experiments)\n3. [(PDF) Abiotic synthesis of amino acids and self-crystallization under prebiotic conditions](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/267743575_Abiotic_synthesis_of_amino_acids_and_self-crystallization_under_prebiotic_conditions)\n4. [Prebiotic synthesis in atmospheres containing CH4, CO, and CO2](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02101642)\n5. [(PDF) A prebiotic Krebs cycle analog generates amino acids with H2 and NH3 over nickel](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/378533181_A_prebiotic_Krebs_cycle_analog_generates_amino_acids_with_H2_and_NH3_over_nickel)\n6. [Prebiotic Synthesis of N-Formylaminonitriles and Derivatives in Formamide](https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/jacs.2c13306)\n7. [Prebiotic synthesis of \u03b1-amino acids and orotate from \u03b1-ketoacids potentiates transition to extant metabolic pathways](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41557-022-00999-w)\n8. [Primordial reductive amination revisited - ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0040403902028630)\n9. [Laurie Barge, Ph.D. Senior Research Scientist, NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory](https://kiss.resources.caltech.edu/uploads/2026/09/etorganics-talk-2-barge.pdf)\n10. [Redox and pH gradients drive amino acid synthesis in iron oxyhydroxide mineral systems](https://www.pnas.org/doi/abs/10.1073/pnas.1812098116)\n11. [(PDF) Effects of Geochemical and Environmental Parameters on Abiotic Organic Chemistry Driven by Iron Hydroxide Minerals](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/346847223_Effects_of_Geochemical_and_Environmental_Parameters_on_Abiotic_Organic_Chemistry_Driven_by_Iron_Hydroxide_Minerals)\n12. [Reaction Mechanisms Quantifying Reductive Amination in Nonenzymatic Amino Acid](https://d-nb.info/1270928767/34)\n13. [Schematic of the branched-chain amino acid (BCAA) biosynthesis pathway...](https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Schematic-of-the-branched-chain-amino-acid-BCAA-biosynthesis-pathway-in-R-eutropha_fig1_264429993)\n14. [Biosynthesis of the Branched-Chain Amino Acids H. E. UMBARGER 27 INTRODUCTION](https://ecosalgenes.frenoy.eu/pdf/782.pdf)\n\n\nKey Citations:\n  1. Prebiotic Synthesis of Hydrophobic and Protein Amino Acids (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.69.3.765)\n  2. ChemI